var e=JSON.parse('[[1201,"The main problem of shallow copying is the shared use of the nested objects.\\nA change of the nested data in the copy can unexpectedly change the original object.\\nThis often leads to bugs that are hard to catch.\\nIt is especially dangerous in an application\'s state and in UI frameworks.","Shallow copying looks safe, but in practice it can lead to hidden errors.\\nUnexpected changes in the data\\nIf you change a nested object in the copy, the original object will change too:\\n\\nThe difficulty of debugging\\nSuch effects are dangerous because:\\n\\n- the change takes place implicitly\\n\\n- the source of the mutation is hard to track down\\n\\n- the error may show itself later\\n\\nProblems in React and other UI frameworks\\nShallow copying can:\\n\\n- break the optimisation of re-renders\\n\\n- lead to the incorrect comparison of states\\n\\n- cause superfluous updates of the UI\\n\\nThe violation of the principle of immutability\\nIn many architectures it is expected that the data:\\n\\n- is not changed directly\\n\\n- is always replaced with new objects\\n\\nShallow copying easily violates that rule.\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe main risk of shallow copying is the hidden mutation of the nested data. It has to be used only with a complete understanding of the object\'s structure.","const state = {\\n  user: { name: \'Alex\' }\\n};\\n\\nconst newState = { ...state };\\nnewState.user.name = \'Bob\';\\n\\n// state.user.name is also \'Bob\'"],[1202,"`structuredClone` is a built-in API for the deep copying of objects.\\nIt correctly copies many data types that `JSON.stringify` does not support.\\n`structuredClone` can work with circular references.\\nIt thereby preserves the structure of the data without a conversion into a string.","`structuredClone` appeared as the standard way of safe deep copying of data.\\nThe definition\\n`structuredClone` is a built-in JavaScript function that creates a deep copy of an object using the structured cloning algorithm.\\nWhat it can do\\nUnlike the JSON approach, `structuredClone`:\\n\\n- supports circular references\\n\\n- correctly copies a `Date`\\n\\n- works with `Map` and `Set`\\n\\n- preserves `ArrayBuffer` and `TypedArray`\\n\\nThe limitations\\nDespite the advantages, there are nuances:\\n\\n- functions are not copied\\n\\n- DOM nodes are not supported\\n\\n- the prototypes of classes are not preserved\\n\\nThe difference from `JSON.stringify`\\nThe key differences:\\n\\n- there is no loss of data on the serialisation\\n\\n- there is no conversion into a string\\n\\n- correct working with circular structures\\n\\nWhen to use it\\n`structuredClone` is suitable if:\\n\\n- you need a safe deep copy\\n\\n- complex data structures are used\\n\\n- correctness matters rather than compatibility with older browsers\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`structuredClone` is the modern and reliable way of deep copying, which in many cases surpasses the JSON approach.","const original = {\\n  date: new Date(),\\n  map: new Map([[\'a\', 1]])\\n};\\n\\nconst copy = structuredClone(original);"],[1203,"An object can be made immutable by means of JavaScript\'s built-in methods.\\nThe simplest way is to use `Object.freeze`.\\nYou can also apply the immutable approach, creating new objects instead of changing the existing ones.\\nFor deep immutability a recursive freeze or third-party libraries are usually used.","Immutability is an important principle when working with state and predictable data.\\nThe definition\\nAn immutable object is an object whose properties cannot be changed, deleted or added after its creation.\\nThe use of `Object.freeze`\\nThis method:\\n\\n- forbids the change of the existing properties\\n\\n- forbids the addition of new properties\\n\\n- forbids the deletion of properties\\n\\nThe limitation of `Object.freeze`\\nIt matters to remember that:\\n\\n- the freeze works only at the first level\\n\\n- nested objects remain mutable\\n\\nThe immutable approach\\nThe alternative and more flexible variant:\\n\\n- do not change the object directly\\n\\n- create a new object on every change\\n\\nDeep immutability\\nFor complete protection you can:\\n\\n- apply `Object.freeze` recursively\\n\\n- use libraries for immutable data structures\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`Object.freeze` protects an object shallowly. For complete immutability it is better to use the creation of new objects.","const user = Object.freeze({\\n  name: \'Alex\'\\n});\\n\\nuser.name = \'Bob\'; // it will not work\\n\\nconst updatedUser = {\\n  ...user,\\n  name: \'Bob\'\\n};"],[1204,"`Object.freeze` forbids changes to an object\'s properties.\\nIt blocks the addition, the deletion and the change of the properties of the first level.\\nThe freeze is not deep, however.\\nNested objects remain mutable.","`Object.freeze` is often perceived as a way of making an object completely immutable, but that is not entirely so.\\nThe definition\\n`Object.freeze` is a method that makes an object non-extensible and non-writable at the top level.\\nWhat exactly is blocked\\nAfter the freeze:\\n\\n- the value of a property cannot be changed\\n\\n- a new property cannot be added\\n\\n- an existing property cannot be deleted\\n\\nWhy the freeze is not deep\\nNested objects:\\n\\n- are not frozen automatically\\n\\n- remain mutable\\n\\nThe checking of the freeze\\nFor the checking you can use:\\n\\n- `Object.isFrozen`\\n\\n- `Object.isSealed`\\n\\nWhen this is enough\\n`Object.freeze` is suitable if:\\n\\n- the object is flat\\n\\n- the nested structures do not change\\n\\n- basic protection against mutations is required\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`Object.freeze` is shallow protection. For deep immutability additional logic is required.","const config = Object.freeze({\\n  mode: \'dark\',\\n  options: { debug: true }\\n});\\n\\nconfig.options.debug = false; // permissible"],[1205,"In the Event Loop there are several queues of tasks.\\nThe main ones are the macrotask queue and the microtask queue.\\nThey differ in the priority of the execution and in the types of task.\\nMicrotasks are carried out before macrotasks.","JavaScript\'s asynchronous model is built around the Event Loop and the queues of tasks.\\nThe definition\\nThe Event Loop is the mechanism that manages the execution of synchronous code and of asynchronous tasks in JavaScript.\\nThe main queues\\nIn the course of the working the following are used:\\n\\n- The macrotask queue\\n\\n- The microtask queue\\n\\nThe macrotask queue\\nThe tasks that land in it:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- the browser\'s events\\n\\n- I/O operations\\n\\nEvery macrotask is carried out in one pass of the cycle.\\nThe microtask queue\\nThis queue has a higher priority. The following land in it:\\n\\n- `Promise.then`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- `MutationObserver`\\n\\nEvery microtask is carried out before the move to the next macrotask.\\nThe order of the execution\\nThe general order looks like this:\\n\\n- The synchronous code\\n\\n- Every microtask\\n\\n- One macrotask\\n\\n- The painting of the UI\\n\\n- The next iteration of the cycle\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe difference between the queues lies in the priority. Microtasks are always carried out before macrotasks, which it matters to take into account when working with asynchronous code.",null],[1206,"Microtasks and macrotasks are different queues of tasks in the Event Loop.\\nMicrotasks have a higher priority and are carried out first.\\nMacrotasks are carried out one per iteration of the cycle.\\nAn incorrect understanding of their order often leads to unexpected behaviour of code.","In order to work correctly with asynchronous code, it matters to understand which operations land in which queue.\\nThe definition\\n\\n- A microtask is a task of high priority, carried out right after the synchronous code\\n\\n- A macrotask is a task of lower priority, carried out in turn between the repaints of the UI\\n\\nWhat counts as a microtask\\nThe microtasks include:\\n\\n- the handlers of `Promise.then` / `catch` / `finally`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- `MutationObserver`\\n\\nWhat counts as a macrotask\\nThe macrotasks include:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- the handlers of DOM events\\n\\n- network and I/O operations\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nBecause of the different priority:\\n\\n- microtasks can be carried out earlier than expected\\n\\n- the order of the logs can look \\"broken\\"\\n\\n- the UI may go a long time without updating\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nMicrotasks are carried out first and are completely emptied before the move to the macrotasks. This is the key point in the debugging of asynchronous code.","Promise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\'microtask\');\\n});\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'macrotask\');\\n}, 0);"],[1207,"If the microtask queue is constantly replenished, the Event Loop will not move on to the macrotasks.\\nAs a result the UI will not be repainted.\\nThe application may \\"freeze\\" without explicit errors.\\nThis is called macrotask starvation.","Microtasks have absolute priority within the bounds of one iteration of the Event Loop.\\nHow the problem works\\nThe algorithm of the Event Loop is arranged like this:\\n\\n- the synchronous code is carried out\\n\\n- every microtask is carried out\\n\\n- only then is a macrotask carried out\\n\\nIf every microtask adds a new microtask:\\n\\nWhat happens in that case\\nAs a result:\\n\\n- the microtask queue will never empty\\n\\n- the macrotasks will not begin to be carried out\\n\\n- the painting of the UI will not take place\\n\\n- the browser will become unresponsive\\n\\nWhy this is dangerous\\nSuch a situation:\\n\\n- is difficult to debug\\n\\n- causes no errors\\n\\n- looks like a \\"freeze\\" of the page\\n\\nHow to avoid it\\nIn practice:\\n\\n- recursive microtasks are not created\\n\\n- `setTimeout` is used to break the cycle\\n\\n- the logic of `Promise.then` is watched\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nEndless microtasks can completely block an application. The priority of microtasks has to be used knowingly.","function loop() {\\n  Promise.resolve().then(loop);\\n}\\nloop();"],[1208,"The painting of the UI takes place between the execution of macrotasks.\\nBefore the painting the browser always empties the microtask queue.\\nIf the microtasks are carried out for a long time, the UI is not updated.\\nAsynchronous code therefore directly affects the interface\'s smoothness.","The rendering of the UI is closely connected with the Event Loop, but it is a separate stage.\\nThe general order of the execution\\nThe typical order looks like this:\\n\\n- The synchronous JavaScript\\n\\n- Every microtask\\n\\n- The handling of one macrotask\\n\\n- The painting of the UI (if it is required)\\n\\n- The next iteration of the cycle\\n\\nWhy the UI waits for the microtasks\\nBefore the painting the browser:\\n\\n- waits for the JavaScript to finish\\n\\n- empties the microtask queue\\n\\n- makes sure that the state is stable\\n\\nThis guarantees a consistent render.\\nThe effect on performance\\nIf:\\n\\n- the microtasks are carried out for too long\\n\\n- the JavaScript blocks the thread\\n\\nthen:\\n\\n- frames are skipped\\n\\n- the interface \\"jerks\\"\\n\\n- lags in the animations appear\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\nFor a smooth UI people:\\n\\n- avoid heavy microtasks\\n\\n- use `requestAnimationFrame` for animations\\n\\n- split complex computations into parts\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe painting of the UI takes place only after the microtasks have been carried out and between the macrotasks. Control of the queues directly affects the interface\'s responsiveness.",null],[1209,"In JavaScript a function\'s context can be bound in various ways.\\nThe main ways are `bind`, `call` and `apply`.\\nThe context also depends on the way the function is called.\\nArrow functions have a particular behaviour of `this`.","The context of the execution (`this`) in JavaScript is determined not by the place of the function\'s declaration but by the way it is called.\\nThe definition\\nA function\'s context is the value of `this` available inside the function\'s body.\\nThe explicit binding of the context\\nJavaScript provides the methods:\\n\\n- `call` — it calls the function with the `this` and the arguments given\\n\\n- `apply` — it is analogous to `call`, but it takes an array of arguments\\n\\n- `bind` — it returns a new function with `this` bound\\n\\nThe context through the way of the call\\nThe context depends on the form of the call:\\n\\n- an object\'s method -> `this` points to the object\\n\\n- an ordinary call -> `undefined` (in strict mode)\\n\\nArrow functions\\nArrow functions:\\n\\n- have no `this` of their own\\n\\n- take `this` from the outer scope\\n\\nThat is convenient for callbacks and handlers.\\nWhen errors arise\\nProblems with `this` often appear:\\n\\n- when passing methods as callbacks\\n\\n- in event handlers\\n\\n- when working with timers\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe context in JavaScript is governed by the way the function is called. Explicit binding and arrow functions help you avoid errors with `this`.","function greet() {\\n  console.log(this.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst user = { name: \'Alex\' };\\ngreet.call(user);"],[1210,"Some array methods change the array itself rather than returning a new one.\\nThis is called the mutation of the data.\\nMutating methods can cause unexpected bugs, especially when working with state.\\nIt matters to be able to distinguish them from the non-mutating methods.","When working with arrays in JavaScript it matters to understand which methods change the original array.\\nThe definition\\nA mutating array method is a method that changes the contents of the original array rather than creating a new one.\\nThe main mutating methods\\nThe most frequently used ones include:\\n\\n- `push`\\n\\n- `pop`\\n\\n- `shift`\\n\\n- `unshift`\\n\\n- `splice`\\n\\n- `sort`\\n\\n- `reverse`\\n\\nWhy this can be a problem\\nMutations are dangerous because:\\n\\n- the original data changes implicitly\\n\\n- it is harder to track the changes\\n\\n- optimisations and comparisons by reference can break\\n\\nThe non-mutating alternatives\\nInstead of mutations people often use:\\n\\n- `concat`\\n\\n- `slice`\\n\\n- `map`\\n\\n- `filter`\\n\\n- `toSorted` (in the modern versions)\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\nIn business logic and UI code the non-mutating methods are preferable, so that the code is predictable and safe.\\nA brief conclusion\\nMutating methods change an array directly. They have to be used knowingly and carefully.","const arr = [1, 2, 3];\\narr.push(4);\\n// arr is now [1, 2, 3, 4]\\n\\nconst newArr = arr.concat(4);"],[1211,"The chain of prototypes is the mechanism by which JavaScript searches for an object\'s properties.\\nIf a property is not found in the object, the search continues in its prototype.\\nSuch a search continues to the end of the chain.\\nThis mechanism lies at the foundation of inheritance in JavaScript.","JavaScript uses prototypal inheritance rather than the classical kind, as in other languages.\\nThe definition\\nThe chain of prototypes is the sequence of objects along which JavaScript searches for a property or a method.\\nHow the search for a property takes place\\nWhen a reference to a property is carried out:\\n\\n- JavaScript searches for it in the object itself\\n\\n- if it is not found, it searches in the prototype\\n\\n- the process repeats to the end of the chain\\n\\nThe prototype and `[[Prototype]]`\\nEvery object has a hidden property:\\n\\n- `[[Prototype]]` — a reference to another object\\n\\nAccess to it is most often obtained through `Object.getPrototypeOf`.\\nWhere the chain ends\\nIn standard objects:\\n\\n- the last link is `Object.prototype`\\n\\n- after that comes `null`\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThe chain of prototypes:\\n\\n- lets methods be reused\\n\\n- reduces the duplication of code\\n\\n- explains where objects\' methods \\"come from\\"\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe chain of prototypes is the foundation of inheritance in JavaScript and the mechanism of the search for properties.","const user = { name: \'Alex\' };\\nconsole.log(user.toString());\\n// the method is found in Object.prototype"],[1212,"On a reference to a variable declared through `var` before its declaration there will be no error. The variable will exist, but its value will be `undefined`. This happens because of the mechanism of the hoisting of declarations. The initialisation of the value takes place only at the place of the actual declaration, however. Such behaviour often leads to errors that are hard to catch.","In JavaScript the declarations of variables through `var` are lifted (hoisting) to the beginning of the function or of the global scope. This means that the JavaScript engine knows in advance of the variable\'s existence but not of its value.\\nHow this works in practice\\nWhen the code is carried out, JavaScript conditionally does the following:\\n\\n- It declares the variable (`var x`)\\n\\n- It initialises it with the value `undefined`\\n\\n- It carries the code out from the top down\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn fact this is equivalent to:\\n\\nWhy this is considered a problem\\n\\n- The code looks as though an undeclared variable is being used\\n\\n- No error arises, which complicates the debugging\\n\\n- The behaviour differs from that of `let` and `const`\\n\\nConclusion\\n`var` permits the use of a variable before its declaration, but this is considered bad practice. In modern JavaScript it is recommended that you use `let` and `const`.","console.log(a); // undefined\\nvar a = 10;\\n\\nvar a;\\nconsole.log(a);\\na = 10;"],[1213,"On a reference to a variable declared through `let` or `const` before its declaration a `ReferenceError` will take place. The variable exists, but it is not available until the moment of the initialisation. This behaviour protects code from implicit errors. Such a limitation makes code more predictable.","Variables declared through `let` and `const` are also hoisted, but they work differently from `var`. They land in the Temporal Dead Zone.\\nWhat the Temporal Dead Zone is\\nThe Temporal Dead Zone is the period from the beginning of the scope to the line with the variable\'s declaration, during which:\\n\\n- the variable exists\\n\\n- access to it is forbidden\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhy it was made this way\\n\\n- The implicit use of uninitialised variables is excluded\\n\\n- The safety and the readability of the code are raised\\n\\n- Errors are discovered earlier rather than being masked by `undefined`\\n\\nThe difference between `let` and `const`\\n\\n- `let` — the value can be changed\\n\\n- `const` — reassignment is forbidden\\nBoth obey the rules of the TDZ.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`let` and `const` deliberately forbid access to a variable before its declaration. This is one of the key improvements of modern JavaScript compared with `var`.","console.log(a); // ReferenceError\\nlet a = 10;"],[1214,"The Event Loop is not part of JavaScript itself. It is implemented in the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js. JavaScript defines the syntax and the model of execution but does not manage the queues of tasks. It is precisely the runtime environment that is responsible for asynchronicity. The behaviour of the Event Loop can therefore differ in different environments.","JavaScript is a single-threaded language, which in itself cannot work with timers, network requests or events. All of that is provided by the runtime environment.\\nWhere the Event Loop is\\nThe Event Loop is implemented:\\n\\n- in browsers (through the Web APIs)\\n\\n- in Node.js (through libuv)\\n\\nThe JavaScript engine (V8, for example):\\n\\n- carries out the synchronous code\\n\\n- manages the call stack\\n\\nThe runtime environment:\\n\\n- manages the queues of tasks\\n\\n- decides when to hand control back to the engine\\n\\nHow this looks conceptually\\n\\n- The synchronous code is carried out in the call stack\\n\\n- The asynchronous operations are passed to the environment\'s APIs\\n\\n- The callbacks land in the queues (the task queue, the microtask queue)\\n\\n- The Event Loop decides when to carry out the next task\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- It explains the differences between the browser and Node.js\\n\\n- It helps you understand the order of the execution of `Promise`, `setTimeout` and `async/await`\\n\\n- It simplifies the debugging of asynchronous code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Event Loop is a mechanism of the runtime environment rather than of the JavaScript language. An understanding of this separation helps you get to grips with asynchronicity more deeply.",null],[1215,"A closure is directly connected with a function\'s lexical environment. A function in JavaScript remembers the environment in which it was declared rather than the one in which it was called. Thanks to that it can turn to the variables of the outer function even after that function has finished. The lexical environment determines which variables are available to the function. It is precisely that which lies at the foundation of the mechanism of closures.","In order to understand closures, it first matters to get to grips with what a lexical environment is.\\nThe definition\\nThe lexical environment is the internal structure in which JavaScript holds:\\n\\n- the local variables\\n\\n- the function\'s parameters\\n\\n- references to the outer environments\\n\\nEvery function on its creation receives a reference to the lexical environment in which it was declared.\\nHow a closure appears\\nA closure arises at the moment when:\\n\\n- A function is declared inside another function\\n\\n- The inner function uses the variables of the outer function\\n\\n- The inner function carries on existing after the outer one has finished\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example:\\n\\n- `inner` closes over the variable `count`\\n\\n- `count` is held in the lexical environment of `outer`\\n\\n- that environment is not deleted while `inner` is available\\n\\nWhy this works\\nJavaScript does not copy the values of the variables.\\nIt holds a reference to the lexical environment, and so:\\n\\n- the state is preserved\\n\\n- the data is not lost between the calls of the function\\n\\nAn important point\\nA closure is not a separate entity.\\nIt is a consequence of the working of functions and lexical environments.\\nConclusion\\nA closure is a function plus its lexical environment. An understanding of this connection lets you work confidently with state, callbacks and asynchronous code.","function outer() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  return function inner() {\\n    count++;\\n    return count;\\n  };\\n}"],[1216,"Yes, a function can preserve access to the variables of the outer function after that function has finished. This is possible thanks to closures. The variables are not deleted by the garbage collector while there is a reference to them. Such behaviour is used for the holding of state. This is one of the language\'s key mechanisms.","In JavaScript the execution of a function does not mean the automatic deletion of all of its data from memory.\\nWhy the variables are not deleted\\nWhen the outer function finishes its execution:\\n\\n- its call stack is cleared\\n\\n- but the lexical environment may remain\\n\\nIf the inner function:\\n\\n- has been returned\\n\\n- has been passed as a callback\\n\\n- has been saved in a variable\\n\\nthen it carries on referring to that environment.\\nAn example:\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n- `createCounter` has finished\\n\\n- but `value` carries on existing\\n\\n- because the function returned refers to it\\n\\nThe role of the garbage collector\\nThe garbage collector deletes data only when:\\n\\n- there are no references to it\\n\\n- it is unreachable from the code being carried out\\n\\nA closure retains a reference to the variables, and so they are preserved.\\nThe practical significance\\n\\n- the holding of state without global variables\\n\\n- the encapsulation of data\\n\\n- the management of access to values\\n\\nConclusion\\nA function can preserve access to variables after the outer function has finished. This is normal and expected behaviour of JavaScript, which lies at the foundation of closures.","function createCounter() {\\n  let value = 0;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    value++;\\n    return value;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter();"],[1217,"Closures are widely used for the holding of state, the encapsulation of data and work with asynchronous code. They let you hide variables from the outside world. They are often applied in event handlers, timers and callbacks. Closures also lie at the foundation of many design patterns. Practically any modern JavaScript code uses them implicitly.","Closures are not a rare device but a basic tool, which is used constantly, sometimes even imperceptibly.\\nThe holding of state\\nClosures let you preserve state without global variables:\\n\\nThe encapsulation of data\\nThe details of the implementation can be hidden:\\n\\nHere `password` is not available from outside.\\nAsynchronous code\\nClosures are actively used in:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `Promise`\\n\\n- event handlers\\n\\nThe function remembers the values that were current at the moment of its creation.\\nPatterns and libraries\\nClosures are applied in:\\n\\n- modules\\n\\n- factory functions\\n\\n- middleware\\n\\n- React hooks (`useState` and `useCallback`, for example)\\n\\nThe possible problems\\n\\n- memory leaks on incorrect use\\n\\n- the unexpected preservation of outdated data\\n\\nConclusion\\nClosures are used for the management of state, for encapsulation and for asynchronicity. This is a fundamental mechanism, without which modern JavaScript cannot be imagined.","function createToggle() {\\n  let isOpen = false;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    isOpen = !isOpen;\\n    return isOpen;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nfunction createUser(name) {\\n  let password = \\"secret\\";\\n\\n  return {\\n    getName() {\\n      return name;\\n    }\\n  };\\n}"],[1218,"`localStorage` and `sessionStorage` are synchronous stores of data in the browser. The main difference is the lifetime of the data. `localStorage` saves the data between reloads and the closing of the browser. `sessionStorage` is cleared when the tab is closed. Both stores are available only within the bounds of one origin.","The browser provides several mechanisms for holding data, and `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` are the simplest of them.\\nThe shared characteristics\\nBoth stores:\\n\\n- work on the key-value principle\\n\\n- hold the data in the form of strings\\n\\n- are synchronous by their nature\\n\\n- are available through `window`\\n\\n- are isolated by origin\\n\\nThe differences in the lifetime\\n`localStorage`:\\n\\n- the data is saved indefinitely\\n\\n- it remains after a reload of the page\\n\\n- it remains after the closing of the browser\\n\\n- it is suitable for long-lived settings\\n\\n`sessionStorage`:\\n\\n- the data lives within the bounds of one tab\\n\\n- it is cleared when the tab is closed\\n\\n- it is not shared between tabs\\n\\n- it is suitable for temporary state\\n\\nPractical examples\\nThe typical scenarios:\\n\\n- `localStorage` — the theme, the language, the user\'s settings\\n\\n- `sessionStorage` — the steps of a form, temporary flags, the state of a session\\n\\nThe limitations\\nIt matters to remember:\\n\\n- the volume is limited\\n\\n- the data is available from JavaScript\\n\\n- they are not suitable for sensitive information\\n\\nConclusion\\n`localStorage` and `sessionStorage` differ in the lifetime of the data. The choice depends on whether the information has to be preserved between the user\'s sessions.",null],[1219,"Manual testing is carried out by a person, and automated testing by means of code. Manual testing is flexible and fast at the start, but it scales badly. Automated tests require time to be written, but they save resources in the long term. In real projects a combination of both approaches is usually used. Every type of testing solves its own tasks.","Testing is not only about the search for bugs but also about the reduction of the risks of changes in code.\\nManual testing\\nThis is the checking of an application by a person without the use of automatic scenarios.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- it is fast to begin\\n\\n- it is easy to check the UI and the UX\\n\\n- it is suitable for exploratory testing\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- it is expensive when scaling\\n\\n- it is easy to miss regressions\\n\\n- the result depends on the person\'s attentiveness\\n\\nAutomated testing\\nThese are tests written in the form of code and carried out automatically.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- the stable checking of scenarios\\n\\n- it catches regressions well\\n\\n- it is easily integrated into CI/CD\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- the time for the development\\n\\n- the maintenance of the tests when the UI changes\\n\\n- not everything is convenient to automate\\n\\nHow they are combined\\nIn practice:\\n\\n- the critical flows are covered by automated tests\\n\\n- complex UX scenarios are checked by hand\\n\\n- manual testing is used for new features\\n\\nConclusion\\nManual and automated testing do not compete but complement one another. The best result comes from their sensible combination.","test(\'button click\', () => {\\n  // the imitation of a click and the checking of the result\\n})"],[1220,"On the front end unit, integration and end-to-end tests are used. Unit tests check individual functions and components. Integration tests check the interaction of the parts of the system. E2E tests imitate the user\'s behaviour in the browser. Every type of test is needed for its own zone of responsibility.","In front-end development it is customary to divide tests by the level of their coverage and their complexity.\\nUnit tests\\nThis is the most basic level of testing.\\nWhat they check:\\n\\n- functions\\n\\n- hooks\\n\\n- isolated components\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- they are fast\\n\\n- they are simple\\n\\n- they are easy to maintain\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- they do not catch problems of integration\\n\\nIntegration tests\\nThey check how the parts of an application work together.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- a component + an API\\n\\n- a form + validation\\n\\n- several connected components\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- they are slower than unit tests\\n\\n- they give more confidence in the logic\\n\\nEnd-to-End tests\\nThey emulate the actions of a real user.\\nWhat they check:\\n\\n- the navigation\\n\\n- the sending of forms\\n\\n- the working with a real API\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- they are the slowest\\n\\n- they are complex to maintain\\n\\n- they are sensitive to changes in the UI\\n\\nThe practical balance\\nPeople usually use:\\n\\n- many unit tests\\n\\n- a moderate quantity of integration tests\\n\\n- a minimal set of E2E tests for the critical scenarios\\n\\nConclusion\\nDifferent types of test solve different tasks. A strong testing strategy is a balance between speed, reliability and the cost of maintenance.",null],[1221,"Most often there is no time to write automated tests because of limited deadlines and the priority of features over quality. For the business it matters to release the functionality faster, and tests give no instantaneous value. Unstable requirements and frequent changes in the UI also get in the way. Sometimes the team simply lacks the experience or the culture of testing. In the end the tests are postponed \\"until later\\".","The absence of automated tests is almost always connected not with laziness but with the project\'s real limitations.\\nThe main causes\\nBefore writing tests, a team runs into several typical problems:\\n\\n- The pressure of the deadlines\\n\\n- hard deadlines\\n\\n- a focus on delivery rather than on quality\\n\\n- tests are perceived as \\"optional\\"\\n\\n- Frequent changes of the requirements\\n\\n- the UI and the logic change constantly\\n\\n- automated tests break fast\\n\\n- the maintenance of the tests begins to cost a lot\\n\\n- The absence of a mature testing strategy\\n\\n- there are no agreements on what exactly to test\\n\\n- there is no prioritisation of the critical flows\\n\\n- the tests are written chaotically or are not written at all\\n\\n- A lack of expertise\\n\\n- the developers are not sure how to write good tests\\n\\n- the fear of increasing the project\'s complexity\\n\\n- the absence of examples in the code base\\n\\nWhy this is a problem\\nWithout automated tests:\\n\\n- the quantity of regressions grows\\n\\n- releases become risky\\n\\n- developers are afraid of refactoring\\n\\nHow it is usually solved\\n\\n- people begin with tests for the critical scenarios\\n\\n- they add tests gradually\\n\\n- they allow time for testing in the planning\\n\\nConclusion\\nAutomated tests are more often not written because of organisational rather than technical causes. The best approach is to write the minimum of tests for the maximum reduction of the risks.",null],[1222,"WebGL lets you work with 3D graphics straight in the browser, using the GPU. This is a powerful but low-level technology. In practice wrapper libraries are more often used in order to simplify the work. 3D is applied for visualisations, interactive scenes and special effects. Without a real need WebGL is usually not used.","3D graphics on the front end is a niche but increasingly sought-after area.\\nWhat WebGL is\\nWebGL is a browser API for work with 3D graphics through the GPU.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- a low-level API\\n\\n- work with shaders\\n\\n- high performance\\n\\n- a difficult barrier to entry\\n\\nHow people usually work with 3D on the front end\\nIn practice pure WebGL is rarely written.\\nMore often people use:\\n\\n- Wrapper libraries\\n\\n- they simplify the work with scenes\\n\\n- they hide the mathematics and the shaders\\n\\n- they speed the development up\\n\\n- The integration with React\\n\\n- the 3D scene lives separately from React\\n\\n- React manages the state and the events\\n\\n- the rendering goes straight into the canvas\\n\\nThe main difficulties\\n\\n- a high load on the GPU\\n\\n- the complexity of debugging\\n\\n- sensitivity to the device\'s performance\\n\\nWhere WebGL is justified\\n\\n- 3D configurators\\n\\n- the visualisation of data\\n\\n- interactive presentations\\n\\n- game elements\\n\\nConclusion\\nWebGL is a powerful tool, but it is expensive in complexity. It is used only where ordinary DOM and CSS can no longer cope.",null],[1223,"Web3 on the front end most often comes down to work with wallets and smart contracts through JavaScript libraries. The application does not hold the data directly but interacts with the blockchain through RPC or providers. Most of the logic still remains on the client and in the smart contracts. It matters to understand the limitations of speed and of UX. Web3 is more about architecture than about a new syntax.","In front-end development Web3 is above all a way of interacting with a decentralised infrastructure.\\nWhat Web3 experience on the front end usually includes\\nAs a rule a front-end developer works with the following tasks:\\n\\n- the connection of the user\'s wallet\\n\\n- the reading of data from the blockchain\\n\\n- the sending of transactions\\n\\n- the display of the status of the operations\\n\\nHow the interaction is arranged\\nThe front end does not communicate with the blockchain directly but uses:\\n\\n- A provider\\n\\n- the point of entry into the blockchain network\\n\\n- it can be in the browser or remote\\n\\n- JavaScript libraries\\n\\n- they abstract the RPC calls away\\n\\n- they simplify the work with contracts\\n\\n- they hide the details of the data formats\\n\\nThe main difficulties\\n\\n- a high delay in the confirmation of transactions\\n\\n- errors caused by gas and fees\\n\\n- a complex UX for users\\n\\n- the impossibility of the instantaneous rollback of operations\\n\\nWhere Web3 is justified\\n\\n- DeFi applications\\n\\n- NFT platforms\\n\\n- DAO interfaces\\n\\n- projects with a requirement of decentralisation\\n\\nConclusion\\nWeb3 on the front end is not about complex code but about an understanding of the blockchain\'s limitations and the ability to build a comprehensible UX on top of a slow and immutable system.","// an example of a request for data from a smart contract\\ncontract.methods.balanceOf(address).call()"],[1224,"An NFT is a unique token in a blockchain, which represents the right of ownership of a digital object. In web applications NFTs are used to confirm ownership and access to functionality. The front end usually displays the token\'s metadata and interacts with the user\'s wallet. The content itself is often held outside the blockchain. NFTs are more about identification than about the files themselves.","NFTs are often perceived as pictures, but technically that is only a small part of the concept.\\nWhat an NFT is\\nAn NFT (Non-Fungible Token) is:\\n\\n- a unique token\\n\\n- with an individual identifier\\n\\n- written into a blockchain\\n\\nUnlike ordinary tokens, an NFT cannot be replaced with another one of the same kind.\\nWhat is held in an NFT\\nAn NFT usually contains:\\n\\n- a link to the metadata\\n\\n- information about the owner\\n\\n- the token\'s unique ID\\n\\nThe file itself (an image, a video) is most often held:\\n\\n- outside the blockchain\\n\\n- in distributed storage\\n\\nHow NFTs are used on the front end\\nFront-end applications usually:\\n\\n- connect the user\'s wallet\\n\\n- obtain the list of NFTs\\n\\n- display the metadata\\n\\n- check the rights of access\\n\\nThe typical scenarios\\n\\n- digital art\\n\\n- access to closed sections of a site\\n\\n- game items\\n\\n- tickets and passes\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn NFT is a tool for the confirmation of unique ownership. On the front end they are used as a source of rights and of state rather than as a store of content.","// the checking of the ownership of a token\\nconst ownsToken = tokens.includes(tokenId)"],[1225,"Express and Nest are popular Node.js frameworks for the creation of server-side applications. Express is minimalist and gives a lot of freedom, but it requires more manual organisation. Nest offers a stricter architecture and is convenient for large-scale projects. For a front-end developer it matters to understand them at the level of APIs and contracts. Deep knowledge is not always obligatory, but an understanding of the principles is useful.","A front-end developer often interacts with back-end code, so a basic understanding of the server-side frameworks greatly simplifies the work.\\nExpress\\nExpress is a minimalist HTTP framework.\\nThe main particulars:\\n\\n- simple and flexible\\n\\n- a minimal quantity of abstractions\\n\\n- a fast start\\n\\nThe typical scenario:\\n\\n- small services\\n\\n- prototypes\\n\\n- simple APIs\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- the architecture is entirely the developer\'s responsibility\\n\\n- it is harder to maintain large projects\\n\\n- there are no strict rules of structure\\n\\nNest\\nNest is a framework with a clear architecture.\\nThe key ideas:\\n\\n- modularity\\n\\n- dependency injection\\n\\n- the explicit separation of the layers\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- it is well suited for large teams\\n\\n- it is simpler to scale\\n\\n- the code is more predictable\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- a higher barrier to entry\\n\\n- more boilerplate\\n\\nWhy this matters to a front-end developer\\nAn understanding of the back-end frameworks helps you:\\n\\n- agree on the format of the API\\n\\n- understand the server\'s limitations\\n\\n- debug problems at the boundary of the front end and the back end faster\\n\\nConclusion\\nExpress and Nest solve different tasks. For a front-end developer it is enough to understand their philosophy and their effect on the API, without going deeply into the details of the implementation.","app.get(\'/users\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.json(users)\\n})"],[1226,"It is usually advantageous to do the `filter` first, in order to reduce the volume of data for the following steps. `sort` is often carried out closer to the end, because sorting is more expensive in time and at the same time mutates the array if it is not copied. It is logical to do the `map` after the filtering, so as not to compute anything superfluous. `reduce` is more often placed last, when you have to gather the total (a number, an object, a Map and so on).","The order of the operations is chosen so as to do the expensive actions on a smaller volume of data and to avoid superfluous computations.\\nThe definition\\n\\n- `filter` — it selects elements by a condition and returns a new array.\\n\\n- `map` — it transforms every element and returns a new array.\\n\\n- `sort` — it sorts the array in place and returns a reference to that same array.\\n\\n- `reduce` — it folds the array into a single value (a number or an object, for example).\\n\\nThe practical rule by default\\n\\n- `filter` — reduce the array as early as possible.\\n\\n- `map` — transform only what is left after the filter.\\n\\n- `sort` — sort closer to the end, on data that is already prepared (and usually after the `map`, if the sorting depends on a computed field).\\n\\n- `reduce` — the final aggregation or gathering of the result.\\n\\nWhy it is so\\n\\n- `filter` and `map` are linear passes over the array.\\n\\n- `sort` is usually more expensive (comparisons and swaps), so it is better to sort a smaller array.\\n\\n- `sort` mutates the array — so as not to break the original data, a copy is often made.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nIf `toSorted` is not available, make a copy:\\n\\nThe important exceptions\\n\\n- If the `sort` has to take the fields of the original elements into account and the `map` removes those fields, sort earlier or preserve the fields.\\n\\n- If a `reduce` replaces `map` + `filter` (in one pass), that can be faster but worse in readability.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMost often use the order `filter -> map -> sort -> reduce`, and do the sorting safely (through a copy or `toSorted`), so as not to mutate the original array.","const users = [\\n  { name: \\"Ann\\", age: 21 },\\n  { name: \\"Bob\\", age: 17 },\\n  { name: \\"Cody\\", age: 30 },\\n];\\n\\nconst result = users\\n  .filter(u => u.age >= 18)\\n  .map(u => ({ ...u, label: `${u.name} (${u.age})` }))\\n  .toSorted((a, b) => a.age - b.age) // if there is no toSorted - see below\\n  .reduce((acc, u) => {\\n    acc.push(u.label);\\n    return acc;\\n  }, []);\\n\\nconsole.log(result);\\n\\nconst sorted = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);"],[1227,"`split` is a method of a string: it turns a string into an array by a separator. `reverse` is a method of an array: it reverses the array in place. `join` is a method of an array: it glues the elements of the array into a string with a separator. These methods are usually used together — to reverse a string, for example: `str.split(\'\').reverse().join(\'\')`.","These methods are often confused, because they are combined in chains, but they belong to different types.\\nThe definition\\n\\n- `split` (String.prototype.split)\\nIt turns a string into an array of substrings.\\n\\n- `reverse` (Array.prototype.reverse)\\nIt reverses an array and mutates the original array.\\n\\n- `join` (Array.prototype.join)\\nIt glues the elements of an array into a string.\\n\\nWhat types they work on\\n\\n- `split` works on a string:\\n\\n- it returns an array of strings.\\n\\n- `reverse` works on an array:\\n\\n- it returns that same array, but now reversed.\\n\\n- `join` works on an array:\\n\\n- it returns a string.\\n\\nExamples\\nSplitting a CSV string:\\n\\nReversing an array:\\n\\nGluing an array into a string:\\n\\nThe frequent case: reversing a string\\n\\nThe pitfalls\\n\\n- `reverse()` mutates the array — if you need the array in its original order later, make a copy:\\n\\n- `split(\\"\\")` breaks some characters (emoji, for example) into parts, because they may consist of several code units.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`split` is string to array, `reverse` is the reversal of an array (with mutation), `join` is array to string. Together they give convenient transformations, but it matters to remember the mutation of `reverse`.","const s = \\"a,b,c\\";\\nconst parts = s.split(\\",\\");\\nconsole.log(parts); // [\\"a\\", \\"b\\", \\"c\\"]\\n\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3];\\narr.reverse();\\nconsole.log(arr); // [3, 2, 1] (the original array has changed)\\n\\nconst arr = [\\"a\\", \\"b\\", \\"c\\"];\\nconst s = arr.join(\\"-\\");\\nconsole.log(s); // \\"a-b-c\\"\\n\\nconst s = \\"hello\\";\\nconst reversed = s.split(\\"\\").reverse().join(\\"\\");\\nconsole.log(reversed); // \\"olleh\\"\\n\\nconst reversed = [...arr].reverse();"],[1228,"The simplest way is to convert the string into an array, remove the duplicates through a `Set` and gather it back. If you have to remove duplicate characters, use `new Set(str)` and `join`. If you have to remove duplicate words, first split the string by spaces (`split`), apply a `Set`, then `join`. It matters to clarify what exactly counts as a \\"duplicate\\": characters, words or substrings.","The way depends on what exactly is duplicated and how the order has to be preserved.\\nThe definition\\n\\n- Deduplication is the removal of repeating elements, leaving only the unique ones.\\n\\nDuplicate characters (we leave the first occurrence and preserve the order)\\n\\nWhat happens\\n\\n- `new Set(s)` iterates over the string as a sequence of characters and holds the unique ones.\\n\\n- `[...]` turns the `Set` back into an array.\\n\\n- `join(\\"\\")` gathers the string.\\n\\nDuplicate words (the separator is a space)\\n\\nA variant: normalisation (if it matters to remove the superfluous spaces)\\n\\nIf it matters to preserve the case, or on the contrary to ignore it\\nTo ignore the case but preserve the original form of the first word met:\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo remove duplicates in a string a `Set` is most often used:\\n\\n- for characters: `[...] + Set + join(\\"\\")`\\n\\n- for words/tokens: `split(...) + Set + join(...)`\\nThe key thing is to choose the \\"unit of uniqueness\\" (a character, a word, a token) and the separator you need correctly.","const s = \\"abacaba\\";\\nconst unique = [...new Set(s)].join(\\"\\");\\nconsole.log(unique); // \\"abc\\"\\n\\nconst s = \\"cat dog cat bird dog\\";\\nconst unique = [...new Set(s.split(\\" \\"))].join(\\" \\");\\nconsole.log(unique); // \\"cat dog bird\\"\\n\\nconst s = \\"cat  dog   cat\\";\\nconst words = s.trim().split(/\\\\s+/); // several spaces -> one separator\\nconst unique = [...new Set(words)].join(\\" \\");\\nconsole.log(unique); // \\"cat dog\\"\\n\\nconst s = \\"Cat cat DOG dog\\";\\nconst words = s.split(\\" \\");\\n\\nconst seen = new Set();\\nconst unique = [];\\n\\nfor (const w of words) {\\n  const key = w.toLowerCase();\\n  if (!seen.has(key)) {\\n    seen.add(key);\\n    unique.push(w);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(unique.join(\\" \\")); // \\"Cat DOG\\""],[1229,"Yes, a `Map` can use any value as a key: primitives, objects, arrays, functions. Unlike an ordinary object, the keys in a `Map` are not coerced into strings. For objects and functions the comparison of the keys takes place by reference rather than by contents. This makes a `Map` convenient for holding data tied to specific objects.","A `Map` is a collection of key-value pairs in which the key can be of any data type without implicit conversions.\\nThe definition\\nA `Map` is a data structure that holds key-value pairs and compares the keys on the SameValueZero principle (almost like `===`, but with `NaN`).\\nWhat types can be keys\\n\\n- The primitives:\\n\\n- `string`\\n\\n- `number`\\n\\n- `boolean`\\n\\n- `null`\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `symbol`\\n\\n- The reference types:\\n\\n- `object`\\n\\n- `array`\\n\\n- `function`\\n\\nAn example with primitives\\n\\nObject keys and the comparison by reference\\n\\nWhy it is so\\n\\n- Objects are compared by reference rather than by structure.\\n\\n- Even objects identical in their contents are different keys if they are different references.\\n\\nThe difference from an ordinary object\\n\\n- In an object the keys are always coerced into strings.\\n\\n- In a `Map` the key preserves its original type.\\n\\n- A `Map` is safe for object keys and does not conflict with the prototype.\\n\\nAn example of the problem with an object\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `Map` can use any data types as keys and is especially useful when the keys have to be objects or functions and it matters to preserve their identity by reference.","const map = new Map();\\n\\nmap.set(\\"id\\", 1);\\nmap.set(42, \\"answer\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(map.get(\\"id\\")); // 1\\nconsole.log(map.get(42));   // \\"answer\\"\\n\\nconst map = new Map();\\n\\nconst user = { name: \\"Ann\\" };\\nmap.set(user, \\"data\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(map.get(user)); // \\"data\\"\\nconsole.log(map.get({ name: \\"Ann\\" })); // undefined\\n\\nconst obj = {};\\nconst key = { a: 1 };\\n\\nobj[key] = \\"value\\";\\nconsole.log(obj); // { \\"[object Object]\\": \\"value\\" }"],[1230,"A `WeakMap` differs in that its keys can only be objects, and they are held as weak references. If there are no longer any strong references to a key object, it is deleted by the garbage collector together with the value. In a `WeakMap` you cannot iterate over the elements or find out the size. A `Map` has no such limitations and retains the data in memory.","The main difference between a `Map` and a `WeakMap` is their effect on the management of memory.\\nThe definition\\nA `WeakMap` is a collection of key-value pairs in which the keys are objects and do not prevent their collection as garbage.\\nThe limitations of a WeakMap\\n\\n- The keys are objects only.\\n\\n- There are no methods:\\n\\n- `keys()`\\n\\n- `values()`\\n\\n- `entries()`\\n\\n- `size`\\n\\n- It cannot be iterated over.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhy it cannot be iterated over\\n\\n- The objects may be deleted at any moment.\\n\\n- The JS engine is not obliged to report when exactly that will happen.\\n\\n- Iteration would violate the abstraction of the collection of garbage.\\n\\nWhen to use a WeakMap\\n\\n- The holding of metadata connected with objects.\\n\\n- A cache that should not lead to memory leaks.\\n\\n- Private data tied to instances.\\n\\nThe typical case\\n\\nWhen you need a Map\\n\\n- You need iteration over the data.\\n\\n- The keys are primitives.\\n\\n- It matters to manage the lifetime of the data explicitly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `WeakMap` is used for safe work with memory and objects, and a `Map` for universal collections with full access and iteration.","const weakMap = new WeakMap();\\n\\nlet user = { name: \\"Ann\\" };\\nweakMap.set(user, \\"metadata\\");\\n\\nuser = null;\\n// the object may be deleted by the garbage collector\\n\\nconst privateData = new WeakMap();\\n\\nfunction User(name) {\\n  privateData.set(this, { name });\\n}\\n\\nUser.prototype.getName = function () {\\n  return privateData.get(this).name;\\n};"],[1231,"A counter through a closure is implemented by a function that holds the state in an outer variable and returns an inner function. That inner function has access to the variable even after the outer function has finished. The state thereby becomes private and is not available directly from outside. Every call of the factory function creates an independent counter.","A closure lets a function \\"remember\\" the variables from the scope in which it was created.\\nThe definition\\nA closure is a function together with the lexical environment in which it was declared.\\nThe basic implementation of a counter\\n\\nWhat happens here\\n\\n- `count` is a local variable of `createCounter`.\\n\\n- The function returned has access to `count`.\\n\\n- `count` is not deleted after `createCounter` has finished.\\n\\nSeveral independent counters\\n\\nAn extended variant with methods\\n\\nWhy this is useful\\n\\n- The state is encapsulated.\\n\\n- There are no global variables.\\n\\n- The behaviour is predictable and testable.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA counter through a closure is the classic example of the use of lexical scope for holding private state and for the creation of independent instances of logic.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    count += 1;\\n    return count;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter();\\n\\ncounter(); // 1\\ncounter(); // 2\\ncounter(); // 3\\n\\nconst c1 = createCounter();\\nconst c2 = createCounter();\\n\\nc1(); // 1\\nc1(); // 2\\nc2(); // 1\\n\\nfunction createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  return {\\n    inc() {\\n      count += 1;\\n      return count;\\n    },\\n    reset() {\\n      count = 0;\\n    },\\n    get() {\\n      return count;\\n    }\\n  };\\n}"],[1232,"The `??` operator returns the right operand only if the left one is equal to `null` or `undefined`. In every other case the left value is returned. Unlike `||`, it does not count `0`, `false` or an empty string as erroneous values. This makes `??` safe for the setting of default values.","`??` solves the typical problem of the `||` operator, which substitutes the default value on any falsy value.\\nThe definition\\nNullish coalescing (`??`) is an operator that returns the right operand if the left one is equal to `null` or `undefined`.\\nThe basic example\\n\\nFor a comparison with `||`:\\n\\nWhen `??` is especially useful\\n\\n- The value `0` is valid.\\n\\n- An empty string `\\"\\"` has a meaning.\\n\\n- `false` is a correct logical state.\\n\\nAn example with settings\\n\\nThe limitations of the use\\n\\n- `??` cannot be mixed with `||` or `&&` without brackets.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`??` is worth using for the setting of default values when it matters to distinguish `null`/`undefined` from other permissible values.","const value = 0;\\nconst result = value ?? 10;\\n\\nconsole.log(result); // 0\\n\\nconst result = value || 10;\\nconsole.log(result); // 10\\n\\nfunction init(options) {\\n  const timeout = options.timeout ?? 3000;\\n  const debug = options.debug ?? false;\\n\\n  // ...\\n}\\n\\n// An error\\na ?? b || c;\\n\\n// Correct\\na ?? (b || c);"],[1233,"`spread` is used for the unfolding of the elements of an array or an object. `rest` is used for the gathering of elements into an array or an object. Visually it is one and the same `...`, but the meaning depends on the place of the use. `spread` is more often applied in the copying and the combining of data, `rest` in functions\' parameters and in destructuring","The `...` operator has two different semantic meanings.\\nThe definition\\n\\n- Spread — it unfolds the elements of a structure.\\n\\n- Rest — it gathers several elements into one variable.\\n\\nThe spread operator\\nIt is used in:\\n\\n- The copying of arrays and objects.\\n\\n- The combining of data.\\n\\n- The passing of arguments into a function.\\n\\nThe passing of arguments:\\n\\nThe rest operator\\nIt is used in:\\n\\n- A function\'s parameters.\\n\\n- Destructuring.\\n\\nIn destructuring:\\n\\nAn important nuance\\n\\n- Spread creates a shallow copy.\\n\\n- Nested objects are copied by reference.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSpread means \\"unfold\\", rest means \\"gather\\". One syntax, but different tasks and contexts of application.","const arr = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst copy = [...arr];\\n\\nconst obj = { a: 1 };\\nconst extended = { ...obj, b: 2 };\\n\\nconst nums = [1, 2, 3];\\nMath.max(...nums);\\n\\nfunction sum(...numbers) {\\n  return numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\\n}\\n\\nconst [first, ...rest] = [1, 2, 3];"],[1234,"Template literals are strings in backticks (```), which support the interpolation of expressions and multi-line text. They let you insert variables and expressions straight into a string through `${}`. This makes code shorter and more readable compared with concatenation. They are also convenient for HTML templates and for logging.","Template literals simplify work with strings and the formatting of text.\\nThe definition\\nTemplate literals are a JavaScript string syntax that uses backticks and supports the interpolation of expressions.\\nThe interpolation of values\\n\\nComputations inside a string\\n\\nMulti-line strings\\n\\nThe typical scenarios of use\\n\\n- The forming of messages and logs.\\n\\n- The generation of HTML or CSS.\\n\\n- Work with localisation.\\n\\n- The raising of the code\'s readability.\\n\\nTagged template literals (briefly)\\nTemplate literals can be passed into a function for the custom handling of the string — for i18n or escaping, for example — but this is used more rarely.\\nConclusion\\nTemplate literals make strings flexible, readable and convenient, especially in the dynamic forming of text and templates.","const name = \\"Alex\\";\\nconst age = 30;\\n\\nconst text = `User ${name} is ${age} years old`;\\n\\nconst a = 2;\\nconst b = 3;\\n\\nconst result = `Sum: ${a + b}`;\\n\\nconst html = `\\n  <div>\\n    <span>Hello</span>\\n  </div>\\n`;"],[1235,"Microtasks are tasks of a higher priority, which are carried out right after the current synchronous code has finished. They include `Promise.then`, `catch`, `finally`, `queueMicrotask` and `MutationObserver`. Macrotasks are tasks of general scheduling, such as `setTimeout`, `setInterval`, `setImmediate`, event handlers and I/O. The event loop always carries out every microtask first, and only then takes the next macrotask.","To understand the difference between microtasks and macrotasks, it matters to get to grips with how the event loop works.\\nThe definition\\nThe event loop is the mechanism of JavaScript that manages the execution of synchronous and asynchronous code using a queue of tasks.\\nMacrotasks (the task queue)\\nThe macrotasks include:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- `setImmediate` (Node.js)\\n\\n- The handlers of DOM events\\n\\n- I/O operations\\n\\nMicrotasks (the microtask queue)\\nThe microtasks include:\\n\\n- `Promise.then / catch / finally`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- `MutationObserver`\\n\\nThe order of the execution\\n\\n- All of the synchronous code is carried out.\\n\\n- Every microtask is carried out.\\n\\n- One macrotask is carried out.\\n\\n- Again every microtask is carried out.\\n\\n- The cycle repeats.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\nWhy microtasks matter\\n\\n- They guarantee the predictable completion of asynchronous chains.\\n\\n- They let a `Promise` be carried out before the timers.\\n\\n- They are used inside `async/await`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMicrotasks have a higher priority and are always carried out before macrotasks, which is critical for an understanding of the order of the execution of asynchronous JavaScript code.","setTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"timeout\\");\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"promise\\");\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\\"start\\");\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"timeout\\"));\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"promise\\"));\\n\\nconsole.log(\\"end\\");\\n\\nstart\\nend\\npromise\\ntimeout"],[1236,"`Promise.then` is always carried out before `setTimeout`, even if the timer has a delay of `0`. This happens because `then` lands in the microtask queue and `setTimeout` in the macrotask queue. Once the synchronous code has finished, the event loop first empties the microtask queue, and only then takes the next macrotask.","This question is a particular case of the working of the event loop, but it is often asked in interviews.\\nThe definition\\n`Promise.then` is a microtask\\n`setTimeout` is a macrotask\\nAn example\\n\\nThe result\\n\\nA more complex example\\n\\nThe order:\\n\\n- The synchronous code has finished.\\n\\n- `promise` has been carried out.\\n\\n- `timeout` has been carried out.\\n\\n- `promise in timeout` has been carried out.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`Promise.then` has priority over `setTimeout`, because microtasks are carried out before macrotasks.","setTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"timeout\\");\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"promise\\");\\n});\\n\\npromise\\ntimeout\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"timeout\\");\\n  Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"promise in timeout\\"));\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"promise\\");\\n});"],[1237,"Any `async` function always returns a `Promise`. If the function returns an ordinary value, it is automatically wrapped in `Promise.resolve`. If a `throw` takes place inside, a `Promise.reject` is returned. `await` does not change the type of the function\'s return value.","`async/await` is syntactic sugar over `Promise`.\\nThe definition\\nAn async function is a function that always returns a `Promise`, independently of what is returned inside.\\nExamples of the return of a value\\n\\nEquivalent to:\\n\\nAn example with an error\\n\\nThe use of await\\n\\nIt matters to understand\\n\\n- `await` suspends the execution of the function but does not block the main thread.\\n\\n- The outer code always works with a `Promise`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn `async` function is always a `Promise`, and `await` merely simplifies the work with asynchronous code without changing the model of the execution.","async function getNumber() {\\n  return 5;\\n}\\n\\ngetNumber().then(console.log); // 5\\n\\nfunction getNumber() {\\n  return Promise.resolve(5);\\n}\\n\\nasync function fail() {\\n  throw new Error(\\"Oops\\");\\n}\\n\\nfail().catch(console.error);\\n\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const data = await Promise.resolve(\\"data\\");\\n  return data;\\n}"],[1238,"`async/await` and `.then()` work on top of `Promise` and give identical capabilities. The difference is that `async/await` lets you write asynchronous code in a synchronous style, improving the readability. `.then()` uses chains of callbacks and more often leads to nesting. From the point of view of the execution and the performance they are equivalent.","Both approaches solve one and the same task, but they organise the code differently.\\nThe definition\\n\\n- `.then()` is a method of a `Promise`, which takes a callback that will be carried out after the resolve.\\n\\n- `async/await` is syntactic sugar over `Promise`, which lets you write asynchronous code linearly.\\n\\nAn example with `.then()`\\n\\nThe same example with async/await\\n\\nThe main differences\\n\\n- Readability\\n\\n- `async/await` reads from the top down.\\n\\n- `.then()` is more complex with long chains.\\n\\n- The handling of errors\\n\\n- `async/await` uses `try/catch`.\\n\\n- `.then()` uses `.catch()`.\\n\\n- Debugging\\n\\n- The call stack is simpler to read with `async/await`.\\n\\n- Flexibility\\n\\n- `.then()` is convenient for short chains.\\n\\n- `async/await` is for complex logic.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice between `async/await` and `.then()` is a question of readability and style rather than of capabilities or performance.","fetchData()\\n  .then(data => process(data))\\n  .then(result => save(result))\\n  .catch(error => handleError(error));\\n\\nasync function run() {\\n  try {\\n    const data = await fetchData();\\n    const result = await process(data);\\n    await save(result);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    handleError(error);\\n  }\\n}"],[1239,"`setInterval` runs the handler to a schedule even if the previous request has not yet finished, because of which parallel requests and a superfluous load appear. The intervals also \\"float\\" (drift): if the handler takes a long time, the actual moments of the start shift. `setTimeout` is more convenient for polling, because it lets you start the next request after the previous one has finished and change the delay flexibly (increase it on errors, for example).","Polling is repeating requests to \\"check the state\\". For it it matters to control the concurrency and the frequency.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: Polling is the periodic checking of the server by the client (\\"have new data appeared?\\", for example).\\nThe problems of setInterval in polling\\n\\n- The overlap of requests\\n\\n- If a request takes 3 seconds and the interval = 1 second, then over 10 seconds a batch of requests will accumulate.\\n\\n- An unpredictable load\\n\\n- The server receives bursts, and the client an increase in the consumption of memory and network.\\n\\n- It is harder to do a backoff\\n\\n- On errors or a `429` the pause has to be increased, and with `setInterval` that is less convenient (often you have to `clearInterval` and set it again).\\n\\n- Drift and the queue of events\\n\\n- If the tab is \\"slowing down\\" or the thread is busy, the firings may shift and bunch up.\\n\\nWhy setTimeout is usually better\\n\\n- The next tick after the completion\\n\\n- We start the timer only when we have finished the current request.\\n\\n- A flexible delay\\n\\n- The interval can be changed dynamically: the normal mode, a backoff on errors, a pause when the tab is hidden.\\n\\nAn example of polling with setTimeout (without overlaps)\\n\\nConclusion\\n`setTimeout` for polling is usually preferable, because it gives you control: there are no parallel requests, a backoff is simpler and the management of the frequency is more comprehensible.","let stopped = false;\\n\\nasync function poll() {\\n  try {\\n    const res = await fetch(\\"/api/status\\");\\n    // the handling of res...\\n    if (!stopped) setTimeout(poll, 1000);\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    // on an error the interval can be increased\\n    if (!stopped) setTimeout(poll, 3000);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\npoll();\\n// to stop it:\\nstopped = true;"],[1240,"Primitives (`number`, `string` and `boolean`, for example) hold \\"the value itself\\", and on assignment the value is copied. Non-primitives (objects, arrays, functions) are held as a reference to an object in memory, and on assignment the reference is copied. Changes to an object through one variable are therefore visible in another. Also, the comparison of objects more often compares the references rather than the contents.","The definition\\nA primitive type is a value that is not an object and behaves like a \\"copyable value\\".\\nA non-primitive type is an object (arrays and functions included), which is passed as a \\"reference to one and the same object\\".\\nWhat counts as a primitive and what as a non-primitive\\n\\n- The primitives:\\n\\n- `string`\\n\\n- `number`\\n\\n- `boolean`\\n\\n- `null`\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `bigint`\\n\\n- `symbol`\\n\\n- The non-primitives:\\n\\n- `object` (including `{}`, `[]`, `new Date()`, `Map`, `Set`)\\n\\n- `function` (technically an object too)\\n\\nThe main difference: a copy of the value vs a copy of the reference\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to remember the idea: a primitive \\"has no internal state that can be referred to\\", while an object does.\\n\\n- Primitives are copied \\"as they are\\"\\n\\n- Objects are copied as a reference to one and the same object\\n\\nHow this affects the comparison\\n\\n- Primitives are compared by value:\\n\\n- `5 === 5` -> `true`\\n\\n- Objects are compared by reference:\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\n\\n- If you want to \\"change data safely\\", objects have to be copied (through spread or `structuredClone`, for example).\\n\\n- If you hold state in the UI (React/Redux), it matters to remember: a change of a nested object without the creation of a new reference often breaks the correct update.","let a = 10;\\nlet b = a;\\nb = 20;\\n\\nconsole.log(a); // 10\\nconsole.log(b); // 20\\n\\nconst obj1 = { x: 1 };\\nconst obj2 = obj1;\\n\\nobj2.x = 2;\\n\\nconsole.log(obj1.x); // 2\\nconsole.log(obj2.x); // 2\\n\\nconst a = { x: 1 };\\nconst b = { x: 1 };\\nconst c = a;\\n\\nconsole.log(a === b); // false (different objects)\\nconsole.log(a === c); // true (one and the same reference)"],[1241,"When you assign an object from one variable to another, it is not the object that is copied but the reference to it. In the end both variables point to one and the same object in memory. If you change the object through one variable, the changes will be visible through the other. To obtain an independent copy you have to copy the object explicitly.","The definition\\nThe assignment of an object is an operation that copies the reference to the object rather than creating a new object.\\nWhat happens on the assignment\\nLet us first say it in words: after `b = a` you have not two objects but one object and two variables looking at it.\\n\\n- An example of an \\"alias\\"\\n\\n- Reassignment changes only the reference in the variable\\n\\nHow to make a copy\\n\\n- A shallow copy — the top fields are copied, the nested objects remain references:\\n\\n- A deep copy — the whole tree is copied (when that is genuinely needed):\\n\\nWhere this matters in practice\\n\\n- React state:\\n\\n- You cannot do `state.obj.x = 2` and expect correct updates.\\n\\n- You have to create a new object: `setState(prev => ({ ...prev, obj: { ...prev.obj, x: 2 } }))`.\\n\\n- Redux:\\n\\n- Immutability is needed so that the changes are \\"visible\\" through new references.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe assignment of an object creates a second reference to the same object, so changes \\"leak\\" between the variables. If you need independence, copy the object explicitly (shallow or deep depending on the structure).","const a = { count: 0 };\\nconst b = a;\\n\\nb.count += 1;\\n\\nconsole.log(a.count); // 1\\n\\nconst a = { count: 0 };\\nlet b = a;\\n\\nb = { count: 100 };\\n\\nconsole.log(a.count); // 0\\nconsole.log(b.count); // 100\\n\\nconst a = { user: { name: \\"Ann\\" }, age: 20 };\\nconst b = { ...a };\\n\\nb.age = 21;\\nb.user.name = \\"Bob\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(a.age); // 20\\nconsole.log(a.user.name); // \\"Bob\\" (because user is shared)\\n\\nconst a = { user: { name: \\"Ann\\" } };\\nconst b = structuredClone(a);\\n\\nb.user.name = \\"Bob\\";\\nconsole.log(a.user.name); // \\"Ann\\""],[1242,"Practically every object in JavaScript ultimately inherits from `Object.prototype`. This is the \\"base\\" object in the chain of prototypes, where the common methods are (`toString`, `hasOwnProperty` and so on). `Object.prototype` itself usually inherits from `null`, that is, it is the end of the chain. Primitives are not objects directly, but on a reference to methods they are often temporarily \\"wrapped\\" in a wrapper object.","The definition\\nThe chain of prototypes is the mechanism by which an object can search for properties and methods not only in itself but also in its prototype (`[[Prototype]]`).\\nThe basic idea of the inheritance\\nBefore the enumeration: when you write `obj.toString()`, JS searches for `toString` first in `obj`, then in its prototype, then higher up — until it finds it or runs into the end of the chain.\\n\\n- For ordinary objects the ultimate \\"base\\" is `Object.prototype`\\n\\n- And the \\"last step\\" after `Object.prototype` is `null`\\n\\nAn example of the checking of the chain\\n\\nAnd what about arrays, functions and dates\\nThey have their own prototypes, but the chain still usually comes to `Object.prototype`.\\n\\n- An array:\\n\\nAn important nuance about primitives\\nPrimitives in themselves do not inherit as objects, but methods appear for them through a temporary \\"wrapper\\":\\n\\n- `\\"hi\\".toUpperCase()` works because the string is temporarily turned into a `String` object.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMost objects in JS ultimately inherit from `Object.prototype`, and the end of the chain of prototypes is `null`. This explains why the basic methods are available almost \\"everywhere\\".","const obj = { a: 1 };\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) === Object.prototype); // true\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Object.prototype)); // null\\n\\nconst arr = [];\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(arr) === Array.prototype); // true\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Array.prototype) === Object.prototype); // true"],[1243,"At the end of the chain of prototypes is `null`. The last \\"real\\" object in the chain is usually `Object.prototype`, and its prototype is equal to `null`. When the search for a property reaches `null`, JavaScript ceases the search and returns `undefined`. This is the final point of any prototypal search.","The definition\\nThe end of the chain of prototypes is the moment when an object no longer has a prototype and the further search for properties is impossible.\\nHow the search for a property is arranged\\nLet us first describe it in words: on a reference to a property JavaScript moves \\"upwards\\" along the chain of prototypes.\\n\\n- The object itself is checked\\n\\n- Its prototype is checked\\n\\n- The prototypes of the prototypes are checked\\n\\n- The search stops when the prototype is equal to `null`\\n\\nWhat exactly is the end of the chain\\n\\n- For most objects:\\n\\n- The last object is `Object.prototype`\\n\\n- Its prototype is `null`\\n\\n- This can be checked explicitly:\\n\\nWhat happens if the property is not found\\nIf the property is not found in any object of the chain:\\n\\n- JavaScript reaches `null`\\n\\n- The search ceases\\n\\n- `undefined` is returned\\n\\nThe practical significance\\n\\n- An understanding of the end of the chain helps you:\\n\\n- Explain why a property \\"disappears\\"\\n\\n- Avoid errors in the access to nested methods\\n\\n- This matters in:\\n\\n- Debugging\\n\\n- An understanding of `hasOwnProperty`\\n\\n- Work with custom inheritance\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe end of the chain of prototypes is always `null`, and the last object before it is `Object.prototype`. This guarantees that the search for properties in JavaScript is always finite.","const obj = {};\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)); // Object.prototype\\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Object.prototype)); // null"],[1244,"Strings are primitives, but on a reference to a method JavaScript temporarily wraps the string in a `String` object. That object contains the methods defined in `String.prototype`. After the call of the method the temporary object is deleted at once. Methods on strings therefore look \\"native\\", although strings themselves are not objects.","The definition\\nAutoboxing is the mechanism by which a primitive is temporarily turned into a wrapper object for access to methods.\\nWhat happens on the call of a method on a string\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to understand the general algorithm that the JavaScript engine carries out.\\n\\n- There is a primitive:\\n\\n- On the call of a method:\\n\\n- JavaScript:\\n\\n- Creates a temporary object `new String(\\"hello\\")`\\n\\n- Searches for the method in `String.prototype`\\n\\n- Calls the method\\n\\n- Deletes the temporary object\\n\\nWhere the methods of strings are held\\n\\n- The methods do not lie \\"in the string\\"\\n\\n- They are in `String.prototype`\\n\\n- This can be checked:\\n\\nWhy it was made precisely this way\\n\\n- Primitives remain light and simple\\n\\n- The methods are not duplicated for every value\\n\\n- The single model of prototypal inheritance is preserved\\n\\nA practical nuance\\nAn attempt to write a property into a string does not work:\\n\\nThis happens because the wrapper object is temporary and is destroyed at once.\\nConclusion\\nMethods appear on strings thanks to autoboxing: the primitive is temporarily turned into a `String` object, the methods are taken from `String.prototype`, and then the object disappears.","const str = \\"hello\\";\\n\\nstr.toUpperCase();\\n\\nconsole.log(String.prototype.toUpperCase !== undefined); // true\\n\\nconst str = \\"hi\\";\\nstr.test = 123;\\n\\nconsole.log(str.test); // undefined"],[1245,"`5.toString()` does not work because JavaScript perceives the dot as part of the numeric literal. As a result the parser \\"waits\\" for the fractional part of the number rather than a call of a method. The variant `5..toString()` works because the first dot finishes the number and the second is used for access to the method. After that the number is temporarily wrapped in a `Number` object and the method is called successfully.","The definition\\nA numeric literal is a way of writing a number straight in the code (`5`, `3.14`, `10`).\\nWhat the problem with `5.toString()` is\\nLet us first describe how the parser sees it.\\n\\n- The notation:\\n\\n- The parser reads this as:\\n\\n- the beginning of the number `5.`\\n\\n- the expectation of a fractional part\\n\\n- In the end a syntax error arises\\n\\nWhy `5..toString()` works\\nHere the behaviour is different:\\n\\n- The first dot finishes the number:\\n\\n- The second dot is the operator of access to a property\\n\\n- Then autoboxing kicks in:\\n\\n- a temporary `Number` is created\\n\\n- the method is taken from `Number.prototype`\\n\\nThe alternative correct ways\\nThere are several readable and safe variants:\\n\\n- Through brackets:\\n\\n- Through a variable:\\n\\n- Through `Number`:\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\n\\n- `5..toString()` is permissible, but it looks strange\\n\\n- In practice brackets or a variable are used\\n\\n- It matters to understand that the problem here is in the syntax rather than in the absence of the method\\n\\nConclusion\\n`5.toString()` does not work because of the rules of the parsing of numeric literals, and `5..toString()` works because it explicitly separates the number from the operator of access to the method and lets autoboxing do its work.","5.toString()\\n\\n5.\\n\\nconsole.log(5..toString()); // \\"5\\"\\n\\n(5).toString();\\n\\nconst n = 5;\\nn.toString();\\n\\nNumber(5).toString();"],[1246,"To call a method on a numeric literal correctly, you have to separate the number from the operator of access to the method explicitly. Most often brackets are used — `(5).toString()` — or the number is saved in a variable beforehand. This lets JavaScript parse the code correctly and temporarily wrap the number in a `Number` object. After that the method is called without errors.","The definition\\nThe call of a method on a numeric literal is a reference to a method from `Number.prototype` on a primitive value of the type `number`.\\nWhy you cannot simply write `5.toString()`\\nFirst it matters to understand the cause of the problem. The JavaScript parser perceives the dot after a number as part of a fractional number rather than as access to a property.\\n\\n- `5.toString()` is a parsing error\\n\\n- `5.` is a permissible numeric literal\\n\\n- An explicit separation is therefore required\\n\\nThe correct ways of calling it\\nThere are several readable and safe variants.\\n\\n- Through brackets:\\n\\n- Through a variable:\\n\\n- Through the `Number` constructor:\\n\\n- Through a double dot (rarely used):\\n\\nWhat happens under the hood\\n\\n- The number remains a primitive\\n\\n- JavaScript temporarily creates a `Number` object\\n\\n- The method is taken from `Number.prototype`\\n\\n- After the call the temporary object is destroyed\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\n\\n- In real code brackets or a variable are preferable\\n\\n- `5..toString()` is permissible, but it lowers the readability\\n\\n- It matters to understand that this is a question of syntax rather than a limitation of the types\\n\\nConclusion\\nMethods on numeric literals are called correctly if you explicitly separate the number from the operator of access to a property. This starts the autoboxing and lets you use the methods of `Number.prototype`.","(5).toString();\\n\\nconst n = 5;\\nn.toString();\\n\\nNumber(5).toString();\\n\\n5..toString();"],[1247,"The keys of an ordinary object can only be strings and `Symbol`s. If other types are used (numbers, objects, booleans), they are automatically coerced into strings. This happens implicitly and can lead to unexpected conflicts of keys. `Symbol` is the only type that is not coerced into a string.","The definition\\nAn object\'s key is the identifier of a property, by which the value is held in and retrieved from the object.\\nWhat types are supported directly\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to stress: not everything you write in square brackets actually remains its own type.\\n\\n- Strings (`string`)\\n\\n- Symbols (`symbol`)\\n\\nWhat happens with the other types\\nEvery other type is coerced into a string.\\n\\n- Numbers:\\n\\n- Booleans:\\n\\n- Objects:\\n\\nWhy `symbol` is a special case\\n\\n- A `Symbol` is not coerced into a string\\n\\n- Every `Symbol` is unique\\n\\n- Symbol keys do not take part in ordinary enumerations (`Object.keys`)\\n\\nA practical nuance\\nIf you need keys \\"of any type\\" without coercion:\\n\\n- Use a `Map`\\n\\n- There the keys can be objects, functions and primitives without conversion\\n\\nConclusion\\nIn ordinary objects the real keys are only strings and `Symbol`s. Every other type is implicitly coerced into a string, which it matters to take into account when designing a data structure.","const obj = {\\n  name: \\"Alex\\"\\n};\\n\\nconst sym = Symbol(\\"id\\");\\nobj[sym] = 123;\\n\\nconst obj = {};\\nobj[1] = \\"one\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj)); // [\\"1\\"]\\n\\nobj[true] = \\"yes\\"; // the key \\"true\\"\\n\\nconst key = {};\\nobj[key] = \\"value\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj)); // [\\"[object Object]\\"]"],[1248,"An object\'s keys are coerced into strings because an object in JavaScript was originally conceived as a \\"string -> value\\" dictionary. Such a decision simplifies the data model and makes the access to properties fast and predictable. This behaviour is fixed in the language\'s specification. The exception is `Symbol`s, added later.","The definition\\nThe coercion of keys into strings is the automatic conversion of a key\'s value into a string when it is used in an object.\\nThe historical cause\\nFirst the explanation in words. JavaScript was created as a simple language for browsers, and an object was regarded as an associative array with string keys.\\n\\n- A simple implementation\\n\\n- Fast access to properties\\n\\n- The minimal complexity of the model\\n\\nHow the coercion works\\nWhen you write:\\n\\nJavaScript does the following:\\n\\n- If `key` is a `symbol`, it is used directly\\n\\n- In every other case a coercion into a string is carried out\\n\\nThe problems that this can cause\\n\\n- Collisions of keys:\\n\\n- Non-obvious keys of the form `\\"[object Object]\\"`\\n\\nWhy this matters for React and the front end\\n\\n- When working with data from an API\\n\\n- In the dynamic forming of objects\\n\\n- In the choice between `Object` and `Map`\\n\\nConclusion\\nObjects\' keys are coerced into strings because of JavaScript\'s architectural decisions and the requirements of the specification. If you have to hold data with keys of any type without conversions, it is worth using a `Map`.","obj[key] = value;\\n\\nconst obj = {};\\nobj[10] = \\"ten\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(obj[\\"10\\"]); // \\"ten\\"\\n\\nconst obj = {};\\nobj[1] = \\"a\\";\\nobj[\\"1\\"] = \\"b\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(obj[1]); // \\"b\\""],[1249,"`debounce` is a technique by which a function is carried out only after a given time has elapsed since the last call. If during that time the function is called again, the timer is reset. In the end the execution takes place once, when the events have \\"fallen quiet\\". This helps reduce the quantity of superfluous operations.","The definition\\nDebounce is the limitation of the frequency of the call of a function so that it is carried out only after a pause in the events.\\nHow debounce works\\nLet us first describe the idea in words: the function \\"waits\\" until the events have stopped happening.\\n\\n- A call of the function takes place\\n\\n- A timer is started\\n\\n- If the call repeats before the timer expires:\\n\\n- the timer is reset\\n\\n- The function is carried out only after a pause\\n\\nA simple example\\n\\nThe typical cases of use\\n\\n- A search as text is typed\\n\\n- The validation of a form\\n\\n- Autosave\\n\\n- Requests to an API when filters change\\n\\nA practical nuance\\nDebounce is almost always used where the last action matters rather than every intermediate one.\\nConclusion\\nDebounce lets you call a function only after the stream of events has ceased, lowering the load and the quantity of superfluous operations.","function debounce(fn, delay) {\\n  let timer;\\n\\n  return function (...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timer);\\n    timer = setTimeout(() => {\\n      fn.apply(this, args);\\n    }, delay);\\n  };\\n}"],[1250,"`debounce` carries a function out only after a pause in the events, while `throttle` does so no more often than once in a given interval of time. Debounce is suitable when the final result matters, and throttle when the regularity of the execution matters. These approaches solve similar tasks but are applied in different scenarios. The choice depends on the behaviour that is expected of the interface.","The definition\\nThrottle is a technique by which a function can be carried out no more often than once in a particular interval of time.\\nThe key difference of the approaches\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to understand the main idea.\\n\\n- Debounce:\\n\\n- it is carried out after the end of a series of events\\n\\n- it ignores the intermediate calls\\n\\n- Throttle:\\n\\n- it is carried out regularly\\n\\n- it limits the maximum frequency of the calls\\n\\nAn example in words\\n\\n- The typing of text:\\n\\n- debounce -> the request will go off after the user has stopped typing\\n\\n- The scrolling of a page:\\n\\n- throttle -> the handler fires once in 100 ms, for example\\n\\nWhy one cannot be replaced with the other\\n\\n- Debounce may \\"never fire\\" if the events come continuously\\n\\n- Throttle guarantees periodic execution\\n\\n- An incorrect choice worsens the UX\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\n\\n- Debounce — forms, search, filters\\n\\n- Throttle — scroll, resize, drag\\n\\nConclusion\\nDebounce and throttle solve one task — the limitation of the frequency — but they do it differently. The choice depends on whether you need the final result or regular execution.",null],[1251,"Debounce is used when events happen often but the result is needed only after the user has finished the action. The typical example is a search as text is typed. It helps lower the load on the application and the server. This makes the interface more responsive and more predictable.","The typical scenarios\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to note: debounce is applied where the last input matters rather than the process.\\n\\n- Search and autocomplete\\n\\n- The filtering of lists\\n\\n- The autosaving of data\\n\\n- The validation of a form\'s fields\\n\\n- The sending of requests when parameters change\\n\\nAn example with a request\\n\\nWhat will happen without debounce\\n\\n- A request is sent on every character\\n\\n- The server is overloaded\\n\\n- The interface may \\"jerk\\"\\n\\nThe connection with React\\n\\n- It is often used together with `useEffect`\\n\\n- It helps you avoid superfluous re-renders and requests\\n\\n- It simplifies the control of the side effects\\n\\nConclusion\\nDebounce is applied in situations with frequent events, when the result is needed only after a pause. This is one of the basic tools of the optimisation of a UI and of network requests.","const debouncedSearch = debounce((query) => {\\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`);\\n}, 300);"],[1252,"`Array.prototype.filter` creates a new array including only those elements for which the callback function returned `true`. The original array is thereby not changed. The callback is called for every element of the array in order. The method is often used for the selection of data by a condition.","The definition\\nArray.prototype.filter is an array method that selects elements by a condition and returns a new array.\\nHow filter works\\nLet us first describe the general idea: `filter` passes over the array and checks every element.\\n\\n- The method takes a callback function\\n\\n- The callback is called for every element\\n\\n- If the callback returns `true`:\\n\\n- the element lands in the new array\\n\\n- If it returns `false`:\\n\\n- the element is skipped\\n\\nThe basic example\\n\\nThe arguments of the callback function\\nThe callback receives:\\n\\n- The current element\\n\\n- The element\'s index\\n\\n- The original array\\n\\nAn important property: immutability\\n\\n- The original array does not change\\n\\n- `filter` always returns a new array\\n\\n- This especially matters when working with state in React\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\n`filter` is the main tool for work with lists of data when you have to select elements without changing the original array.\\nConclusion\\n`Array.prototype.filter` checks the elements of an array in sequence and forms a new array on the basis of a logical condition.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\n\\nconst even = numbers.filter(n => n % 2 === 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(even); // [2, 4]\\n\\nnumbers.filter((value, index, array) => {\\n  return value > 2;\\n});"],[1253,"`this` inside the callback function of `filter` can be `undefined` because the callback is called as an ordinary function rather than as a method of an object. In strict mode (`\\"use strict\\"`) the value of `this` in such functions is equal to `undefined`. The context is not \\"passed automatically\\" into the callback. `this` there therefore often turns out not to be what people expect.","The definition\\nThe context of the execution (`this`) is the object to which a function is bound at the moment of the call.\\nHow the callback in filter is called\\nIt matters first to understand how exactly the callback is called.\\n\\n- `filter` calls the callback itself\\n\\n- The callback is not bound to an object\\n\\n- The call looks conceptually like this:\\n\\nThe behaviour of this\\n\\n- In the ordinary mode:\\n\\n- `this` may point to the global object\\n\\n- In strict mode:\\n\\n- `this === undefined`\\n\\nWhy this often causes errors\\n\\n- `this` from the outer object is expected\\n\\n- But the callback is a separate function\\n\\n- The context is lost\\n\\nThe connection with React\\n\\n- In functional components `this` is not used\\n\\n- In class components such an error is met with often\\n\\n- It therefore matters to manage the context explicitly\\n\\nConclusion\\n`this` inside the callback of `filter` can be `undefined` because the callback is called without a binding to an object, especially in strict mode.","callback(element, index, array);\\n\\n\\"use strict\\";\\n\\n[1, 2, 3].filter(function (x) {\\n  console.log(this); // undefined\\n  return x > 1;\\n});"],[1254,"The correct context can be passed into a callback in several ways: use an arrow function, bind `this` explicitly through `bind`, or pass a second argument into the array methods. The most popular and safest way is arrow functions, since they have no `this` of their own. The choice of the approach depends on the style of the code and on the environment.","The main idea\\nBefore enumerating the ways, it matters to remember: `this` depends not on the place of the function\'s declaration but on the way it is called.\\nThe ways of passing the context\\n1. An arrow function\\nArrow functions have no `this` of their own and take it from the outer scope.\\n\\n2. Explicit binding through bind\\nThe context can be bound by hand.\\n\\n3. The second argument of filter\\nThe array methods take a `thisArg`.\\n\\nWhich way is preferable\\n\\n- Arrow functions are the modern standard\\n\\n- `bind` is useful in old code\\n\\n- `thisArg` is met with more rarely, but it matters to know it for interviews\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe context in an array\'s callback has to be passed explicitly. The simplest and most readable way is to use arrow functions, which inherit `this` from the outer scope.","const obj = {\\n  limit: 2,\\n  filterNumbers(arr) {\\n    return arr.filter(n => n > this.limit);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  limit: 2\\n};\\n\\n[1, 2, 3].filter(function (n) {\\n  return n > this.limit;\\n}.bind(obj));\\n\\nconst obj = { limit: 2 };\\n\\n[1, 2, 3].filter(function (n) {\\n  return n > this.limit;\\n}, obj);"],[1255,"A `microtask` is a task of a higher priority, which is carried out right after the current call stack. A `macrotask` is a task of ordinary priority, which is handled in turn between renders. A Promise and `queueMicrotask` create microtasks, while `setTimeout` and DOM events create macrotasks. The difference affects the order of the execution and the render.","The definition\\nA microtask is a task that is carried out right after the current synchronous code has finished.\\nA macrotask is a task that lands in the queue of events and is carried out in the next cycle of the event loop.\\nThe APIs that create microtasks\\nLet us first enumerate the tasks of high priority.\\n\\n- `Promise.then / catch / finally`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- `MutationObserver`\\n\\nThe APIs that create macrotasks\\nNow the tasks of ordinary priority.\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- `setImmediate` (in the Node.js environment)\\n\\n- DOM events (`click`, `scroll`)\\n\\n- `requestAnimationFrame` (a special case, before the render)\\n\\nThe order of the execution\\nThe general scheme looks like this:\\n\\n- The synchronous code is carried out\\n\\n- Every microtask is carried out\\n\\n- The render is carried out\\n\\n- One macrotask is carried out\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\n\\n- Microtasks have priority over macrotasks\\n\\n- The incorrect use of a Promise can \\"push back\\" the render\\n\\n- This matters in the optimisation of a UI\\n\\nConclusion\\nMicrotasks and macrotasks are different queues with a different priority. A knowledge of the APIs that create them helps you predict the order of the execution of code.","Promise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"microtask\\");\\n});\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"macrotask\\");\\n}, 0);"],[1256,"Yes, microtasks can block the browser\'s render. The browser carries out every microtask before moving on to the render. If microtasks constantly add new microtasks, the render is postponed. This state is called starvation.","How the browser decides when to render\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to understand the order of the steps.\\n\\n- The current JavaScript is carried out\\n\\n- Every microtask is carried out\\n\\n- Only after that is a render possible\\n\\nHow the blocking arises\\nIf a new microtask is created inside a microtask:\\n\\n- The microtask queue never becomes empty\\n\\n- The browser does not reach the step of the render\\n\\n- The UI \\"freezes\\"\\n\\nWhy macrotasks do not do that\\n\\n- After every macrotask the browser can carry out a render\\n\\n- Macrotasks have no priority over the render\\n\\n- `setTimeout` is therefore safer for long loops\\n\\nThe practical significance for the front end\\n\\n- Be careful with recursive Promises\\n\\n- Do not do heavy computations in a microtask\\n\\n- For lengthy processes use:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `requestIdleCallback`\\n\\n- Web Workers\\n\\nConclusion\\nMicrotasks can block the render if the queue never empties. This is a rare but important trap, which interviewers like to ask about.","function loop() {\\n  Promise.resolve().then(loop);\\n}\\n\\nloop();"],[1257,"An analogue of `Promise.all` can be implemented by creating a new Promise and manually tracking the completion of all of the Promises passed to it. You have to save the results in an array in the correct order and count the number of operations finished. If at least one Promise is rejected, the resulting Promise has to pass into the rejected state at once. This demonstrates an understanding of the basic model of a Promise.","The definition\\nPromise.all is a method that returns a Promise resolving with an array of results when every input Promise has finished successfully.\\nThe main requirements of the implementation\\nBefore the code it matters to understand the conditions.\\n\\n- Preserve the order of the results\\n\\n- Resolve only after every Promise has finished\\n\\n- Reject on the first error\\n\\n- Support non-Promise values\\n\\nA simplified implementation\\n\\nWhy Promise.resolve matters\\n\\n- It lets ordinary values be handled\\n\\n- It makes the behaviour compatible with `Promise.all`\\n\\n- It simplifies the logic\\n\\nWhere this is useful\\n\\n- An understanding of the internal arrangement of a Promise\\n\\n- Interviews at the Middle+ level\\n\\n- The debugging of complex asynchronous scenarios\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe manual implementation of `Promise.all` shows a deep understanding of asynchronicity: the queues of tasks, the handling of errors and the synchronisation of several Promises.","function promiseAll(promises) {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    const results = [];\\n    let completed = 0;\\n\\n    promises.forEach((p, index) => {\\n      Promise.resolve(p)\\n        .then(value => {\\n          results[index] = value;\\n          completed += 1;\\n\\n          if (completed === promises.length) {\\n            resolve(results);\\n          }\\n        })\\n        .catch(reject);\\n    });\\n\\n    if (promises.length === 0) {\\n      resolve([]);\\n    }\\n  });\\n}"],[1258,"`this` does not \\"lie\\" inside a function for ever — it is determined at the moment of the call. If the function is called as a method of an object (`obj.fn()`), then `this` will usually point to `obj`. If you move the method out into a variable and call it as an ordinary function (`const f = obj.fn; f()`), then `this` will be lost: in `strict mode` it will become `undefined`, and without `strict mode` the global object (in the browser that is `window`). An arrow function has no `this` of its own; it takes it from the outer environment.","The definition\\n`this` is the context of a function\'s call, which is determined by the call site (the place or the way of the call) rather than by the place of the function\'s declaration.\\nHow to work out what will be in `this`\\nIt is usually enough to look at the form of the call:\\n\\n- A call as a method: `obj.method()`\\n\\n- `this` -> `obj`\\n\\n- An ordinary call of a function: `fn()`\\n\\n- `this` -> `undefined` in `strict mode`\\n\\n- `this` -> the global object without `strict mode`\\n\\n- An arrow function: `() => {}`\\n\\n- `this` does not change on the call; it is taken from outside\\n\\nExamples\\n1) An object\'s method\\n\\n2) The loss of the context when the method is \\"torn off\\"\\n\\n3) An arrow inside a method (it takes `this` from outside)\\n\\nThe practical sense\\n\\n- The loss of `this` often happens when we:\\n\\n- pass a method as a callback (`onClick={obj.method}`)\\n\\n- destructure a method (`const { method } = obj`)\\n\\n- use `setTimeout(obj.method, 0)`\\n\\nConclusion\\n`this` depends on the form of the call. To avoid surprises, watch whether you are calling the function as `obj.method()` or as `fn()`; for callbacks `bind`, arrows or a wrapper function are often used.","\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst user = {\\n  name: \'Ann\',\\n  show() {\\n    console.log(this.name);\\n  },\\n};\\n\\nuser.show(); // \\"Ann\\" (this = user)\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst user = {\\n  name: \'Ann\',\\n  show() {\\n    console.log(this);\\n  },\\n};\\n\\nconst f = user.show;\\nf(); // undefined (strict mode)\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst user = {\\n  name: \'Ann\',\\n  showLater() {\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      console.log(this.name);\\n    }, 0);\\n  },\\n};\\n\\nuser.showLater(); // \\"Ann\\""],[1259,"The context can be bound through `bind`, `call` or `apply`, through a wrapper (`() => fn.call(obj)`), or by using an arrow function where you need `this` from outside. `bind` returns a new function with `this` (and optionally the arguments) \\"glued on\\". An arrow function has no `this` of its own and takes `this` from the outer scope, so it cannot be \\"rebound\\". `bind` is convenient when you need a reusable function with a fixed `this`, and an arrow when it matters to preserve the outer context (inside a method when passing a callback, for example).","The definition\\nThe binding of the context is a way of making `this` inside a function predictable even if the function is called \\"not as a method\\".\\nThe ways of binding or controlling `this`\\n\\n- `bind`\\n\\n- it fixes `this`\\n\\n- it can partially \\"substitute\\" the arguments (currying)\\n\\n- it returns a new function\\n\\n- `call` / `apply`\\n\\n- they call the function at once\\n\\n- they set `this` for the specific call\\n\\n- the difference: `call(fn, a, b)`, `apply(fn, [a, b])`\\n\\n- An arrow wrapper\\n\\n- it preserves the outer `this`\\n\\n- it lets you call explicitly with the context you need\\n\\nHow `bind` differs from an arrow function\\n1) `bind` \\"glues\\" `this` to the function\\n\\n2) An arrow\'s `this` is lexical and does not change\\n\\n3) An important nuance: an arrow cannot be \\"rebound\\"\\n\\nThe practical cases\\n\\n- `bind` is useful when:\\n\\n- you have to pass a function as a callback and not lose `this`\\n\\n- you need a function that you will reuse many times\\n\\n- An arrow is useful when:\\n\\n- you are writing a callback inside a method and want to preserve the method\'s `this`\\n\\n- you do not want to think about `this` inside nested functions\\n\\nConclusion\\n`bind` creates a new function with a fixed `this` and is suitable for reusable callbacks. An arrow creates no `this` of its own and simply \\"inherits\\" the outer context — ideal for nested handlers inside methods.","\'use strict\';\\n\\nfunction show() {\\n  console.log(this.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst user = { name: \'Ann\' };\\n\\nconst bound = show.bind(user);\\nbound(); // \\"Ann\\"\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst user = {\\n  name: \'Ann\',\\n  showLater() {\\n    const arrow = () => console.log(this.name);\\n    arrow();\\n  },\\n};\\n\\nuser.showLater(); // \\"Ann\\"\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst user1 = { name: \'Ann\' };\\nconst user2 = { name: \'Bob\' };\\n\\nconst arrow = () => console.log(this); // this is taken from outside (in a module usually undefined)\\nconst bound = arrow.bind(user1);\\n\\nbound(); // it will not become user1"],[1260,"Hoisting is when declarations are \\"handled\\" before the code is carried out. With `var` the declaration is lifted and the variable is immediately initialised as `undefined`, so it can be referred to before the line of the declaration (you will get `undefined`). With `let` and `const` the declaration is also lifted, but until the actual initialisation the variable is in the TDZ (temporal dead zone), and access to it before the declaration causes a `ReferenceError`. `const` additionally requires initialisation right at the declaration.","The definition\\nHoisting is behaviour of the engine by which declarations are taken into account before the code is carried out. Important: it is not the value that is \\"lifted\\" but the fact of the declaration.\\n`var`: the lifting + initialisation with `undefined`\\n\\n- `var` has function scope\\n\\n- on entry into the function the variable exists and is equal to `undefined`\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThis is equivalent in sense to:\\n\\n`let` and `const`: the lifting of the declaration + the TDZ\\n\\n- `let`/`const` have block scope\\n\\n- the variable exists \\"in the block\\" but is not available until the line of the declaration\\n\\n- an attempt to read it before the declaration -> `ReferenceError`\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n`const` additionally\\n\\n- `const` cannot be declared without a value\\n\\n- reassignment is forbidden (but the object inside may be mutable)\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhy this matters in real code\\n\\n- `var` can \\"mask\\" errors, returning `undefined` where a variable was expected\\n\\n- `let`/`const` give safer behaviour: the error is visible at once\\n\\n- in loops and blocks `let` often prevents bugs with \\"floating\\" values\\n\\nConclusion\\n`var` is lifted and initialised with `undefined`, so access \\"before the declaration\\" is possible. `let`/`const` are also lifted, but until the initialisation they are in the TDZ, so access before the declaration leads to a `ReferenceError` — this makes code safer.","\'use strict\';\\n\\nconsole.log(x); // undefined\\nvar x = 10;\\nconsole.log(x); // 10\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nvar x;           // the declaration has been lifted\\nconsole.log(x);  // undefined\\nx = 10;          // the assignment has stayed in place\\nconsole.log(x);\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconsole.log(a); // ReferenceError\\nlet a = 10;\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst n = 1;\\n// n = 2; // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable"],[1261,"`let` and `const` are lifted (hoisting does take place) but are not initialised at once. Until the line of the declaration the variable is in the TDZ, and any reference to it leads to a `ReferenceError`. This was done deliberately, in order to avoid hidden bugs in which code reads a variable before its logical declaration. Unlike `var`, where `undefined` is returned before the declaration, `let`/`const` signal an error at once.","The definition\\nThe TDZ (temporal dead zone) is the span of time from the beginning of the scope to the line of the initialisation of a `let` or `const` variable, in which the variable exists but is not available.\\nWhat happens step by step\\n\\n- The engine sees the `let`/`const` declaration and reserves the name in the scope\\n\\n- Until the line of the declaration the variable is in the TDZ\\n\\n- Any attempt to:\\n\\n- read the value\\n\\n- use it in an expression\\nleads to a `ReferenceError`\\n\\nAn example with `let`\\n\\nFor a comparison with `var`\\n\\nWhy it was made this way\\n\\n- So that errors are explicit rather than masked\\n\\n- So that code reads from the top down without \\"magic\\"\\n\\n- To reduce the quantity of bugs in complex blocks and loops\\n\\nThe particular feature of `const`\\n\\n- `const` also has a TDZ\\n\\n- It requires initialisation at once\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `ReferenceError` with `let`/`const` is a protective mechanism. It makes you write code in which variables are used only after an explicit declaration, making the behaviour predictable.","\'use strict\';\\n\\nconsole.log(a); // ReferenceError\\nlet a = 10;\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconsole.log(b); // undefined\\nvar b = 10;\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nconst x; // SyntaxError"],[1262,"`then` is called on the successful completion of a Promise, `catch` on an error, and `finally` always, independently of the result. `finally` is carried out after `then` or `catch`, but it does not change the value unless it itself throws an error. All of these callbacks are carried out as microtasks. The order follows the chain strictly.","The basic order of the execution\\nA Promise passes through one of the states:\\n\\n- `fulfilled` -> `then` is called\\n\\n- `rejected` -> `catch` is called\\n\\n- After that `finally` is always carried out\\n\\nAn example of a successful completion\\n\\nThe order of the output:\\n\\n- `then 1`\\n\\n- `finally`\\n\\nAn example with an error\\n\\nThe order of the output:\\n\\n- `catch err`\\n\\n- `finally`\\n\\nAn important nuance about `finally`\\n\\n- `finally` does not receive the result\\n\\n- `finally` does not change the chain if it:\\n\\n- returns no Promise\\n\\n- throws no error\\n\\nConclusion\\n`then` and `catch` handle the result or the error, and `finally` is the place for the cleanup of resources. It is always carried out and does not affect the data unless it does so explicitly.","Promise.resolve(1)\\n  .then(v => {\\n    console.log(\'then\', v);\\n    return v + 1;\\n  })\\n  .finally(() => {\\n    console.log(\'finally\');\\n  });\\n\\nPromise.reject(\'err\')\\n  .then(() => {\\n    console.log(\'then\');\\n  })\\n  .catch(e => {\\n    console.log(\'catch\', e);\\n  })\\n  .finally(() => {\\n    console.log(\'finally\');\\n  });\\n\\nPromise.resolve(1)\\n  .finally(() => 100)\\n  .then(v => console.log(v)); // 1"],[1263,"Yes, inside a `then` an error can be thrown by means of `throw`. Such an error automatically turns the Promise into a `rejected` one. It will be caught by the nearest `catch` further down the chain. In essence a `throw` inside a `then` is equivalent to `return Promise.reject(error)`.","How an error in a `then` works\\nIf inside a `then`:\\n\\n- an error is thrown (`throw`)\\n\\n- `Promise.reject(...)` is returned\\n\\nthen the chain switches into the error mode.\\nAn example with `throw`\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n- the `catch` will catch the error `\\"boom\\"`\\n\\nThe equivalent through `reject`\\n\\nWhat if there is no `catch`\\n\\n- The error will \\"fall through\\" further down the chain\\n\\n- If there is not a single `catch`, an `UnhandledPromiseRejection` will appear\\n\\nThe connection with `finally`\\n\\n- `finally` will be carried out before the next `catch`\\n\\n- But it will not swallow the error unless it handles it itself\\n\\nConclusion\\nErrors inside a `then` are normal and expected practice. They are automatically turned into a `rejected` Promise and are handled by the nearest `catch`, which makes chains of Promises predictable and convenient.","Promise.resolve(1)\\n  .then(v => {\\n    throw new Error(\'boom\');\\n  })\\n  .catch(e => {\\n    console.log(e.message);\\n  });\\n\\nPromise.resolve(1)\\n  .then(() => {\\n    return Promise.reject(\'fail\');\\n  })\\n  .catch(e => console.log(e));"],[1264,"Technically one `catch` at the end of the chain is enough. It will intercept any error that has arisen above. Additional `catch`es are needed only when an error has to be handled locally and the execution of the chain continued. Superfluous `catch`es without need complicate code and worsen its readability.","The basic principle\\nA chain of Promises propagates an error downwards until it meets a `catch`.\\n\\n- An error has arisen in a `then`\\n\\n- Every subsequent `then` is skipped\\n\\n- The first suitable `catch` below is carried out\\n\\nThe minimally sufficient variant\\n\\n- Any error from `fetchData`, `parse` or `save` will land in `handleError`\\n\\nWhen several `catch`es make sense\\n\\n- You have to handle an error and continue the chain\\n\\n- Different stages require different logic of handling\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nAn important nuance\\nIf a `catch`:\\n\\n- has handled the error\\n\\n- has returned a value\\n\\nthen the chain is considered successful again.\\nConclusion\\nOne `catch` at the end is the standard and the best practice. Several `catch`es are used knowingly, when you have to recover from an error and continue the execution.","fetchData()\\n  .then(parse)\\n  .then(save)\\n  .catch(handleError);\\n\\nfetchData()\\n  .catch(() => getFallbackData())\\n  .then(process)\\n  .catch(logError);"],[1265,"`let` in a loop creates a new variable on every iteration. Every closure therefore \\"remembers\\" its own value. Unlike `var`, where one shared variable is used for the whole loop, `let` prevents the typical error with identical values. This behaviour is written into the language\'s specification.","What happens with `let` in a loop\\nWhen `let` is used:\\n\\n- Every iteration of the loop has its own scope\\n\\n- The variable is recreated anew\\n\\n- The closure captures a unique value\\n\\nAn example with `let`\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n- `0`\\n\\n- `1`\\n\\n- `2`\\n\\nThe same thing with `var`\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n- `3`\\n\\n- `3`\\n\\n- `3`\\n\\nWhy this happens\\n\\n- `var` has one shared variable\\n\\n- By the moment the `setTimeout` is carried out the loop has already finished\\n\\n- `let` creates a new binding on every iteration\\n\\nThe practical effect\\n\\n- `let` makes code safer\\n\\n- no IIFEs or additional wrappers are needed\\n\\n- the behaviour is intuitively what you expect\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe different values with `let` are not \\"magic\\" but the result of block scope and the correct working of closures.","for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(i);\\n  }, 0);\\n}\\n\\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(i);\\n  }, 0);\\n}"],[1266,"A counter function will return an increasing value on every call. This is possible because the inner function preserves access to the variable from the outer function. Such a variable is not destroyed after the execution but carries on living in the closure. Every call uses one and the same preserved state.","The definition\\nA closure is a function that has access to the variables of an outer scope even after the execution of that scope has finished.\\nAn example of a counter\\n\\nThe behaviour on the calls\\n\\nWhat matters here\\n\\n- `count` is created once\\n\\n- It is not available from outside directly\\n\\n- Every call works with one and the same variable\\n\\nWhy this is useful\\n\\n- The encapsulation of state\\n\\n- The absence of global variables\\n\\n- It is often used in:\\n\\n- counters\\n\\n- caching\\n\\n- factories of functions\\n\\nConclusion\\nA counter function with a closure returns an increasing value because the inner function preserves and changes the state between the calls.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    count++;\\n    return count;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter();\\n\\ncounter(); // 1\\ncounter(); // 2\\ncounter(); // 3"],[1267,"Several calls of a function can refer to one and the same variable if that variable is in an outer lexical environment. A closure does not copy the variable\'s value but preserves a reference to it. Every function created in one environment therefore works with one and the same state. A change of the variable in one place will be visible in all of the others.","The definition\\nThe lexical environment is an internal structure of JavaScript in which the variables available to a function at the moment of its creation are held.\\nHow a shared variable arises\\n\\n- The outer function creates a variable\\n\\n- The inner function uses that variable\\n\\n- The outer function finishes its execution\\n\\n- The variable is not deleted, because there is a reference to it\\n\\nAn example of a shared variable\\n\\nWhy this is one and the same variable\\n\\n- `value` was created once\\n\\n- `fn1` and `fn2` point to one function\\n\\n- The closure contains a reference to one environment\\n\\nA frequent source of bugs\\n\\n- Every call works with one state\\n\\n- Without explicit control this can be unexpected\\n\\nConclusion\\nClosures work with a reference to a variable rather than with its copy. Several calls can therefore change one and the same state.","function createFn() {\\n  let value = 0;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    value++;\\n    console.log(value);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst fn1 = createFn();\\nconst fn2 = fn1;\\n\\nfn1(); // 1\\nfn2(); // 2\\n\\nlet shared = 0;\\n\\nfunction inc() {\\n  shared++;\\n}\\n\\ninc();\\ninc();"],[1268,"The time complexity shows how the time of an algorithm\'s execution grows as the input data increases. Big O notation is usually used, in which only the dominant factor of the growth is taken into account. Constants and small terms are discarded. The main focus is on the number of operations in the worst case.","The definition\\nTime complexity (Big O) is an assessment of the growth of the number of an algorithm\'s operations depending on the size of the input data `n`.\\nThe basic steps of the determination of the complexity\\n\\n- Determine what the size of the input depends on (`n`)\\n\\n- Count the number of operations\\n\\n- Keep the dominant term\\n\\n- Ignore the constants\\n\\nExamples\\nO(1) — constant\\n\\nO(n) — linear\\n\\nO(n²) — quadratic\\n\\nThe important nuances\\n\\n- Nested loops -> the multiplication of the complexities\\n\\n- Sequential loops -> the complexities are added, but the dominant one remains\\n\\n- Most often the worst case is assessed\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe determination of the time complexity is the ability to abstract away from the details of the implementation and look at the growth of the number of operations as `n` increases.","arr[0];\\n\\nfor (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n  // an operation\\n}\\n\\nfor (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n  for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {\\n    // an operation\\n  }\\n}"],[1269,"The operations of access to a dictionary object (an `Object` or a `Map`) usually have O(1). This is connected with the use of hash tables. In the worst case, however, the complexity can degrade to O(n) because of collisions. In practice for most tasks the access is considered constant.","What a dictionary object is\\nAn object or a `Map` is often used as a structure:\\n\\n- key -> value\\n\\n- fast access by the key\\n\\nThe average case\\n\\n- The key is hashed\\n\\n- By the hash the cell is found\\n\\n- The access takes place in O(1)\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe worst case\\n\\n- Many keys with the same hash\\n\\n- Collisions arise\\n\\n- The search can become linear -> O(n)\\n\\nWhy in interviews people say O(1)\\n\\n- Modern implementations minimise collisions\\n\\n- The worst case is rarely taken into account\\n\\n- It matters to understand the asymptotics rather than the engine\'s implementation\\n\\nConclusion\\nSolutions with a dictionary object work on average in O(1), which makes them popular for caches, counts and fast searches.","const dict = {};\\ndict[\'id\'] = 10;\\nconsole.log(dict[\'id\']); // O(1)"],[1270,"To count every element, nested arrays included, you have to traverse the structure recursively. If an element is an array, we go inside and carry on counting. If it is an ordinary value, we increment the counter. Such an approach correctly takes any depth of nesting into account.","The general idea of the solution\\nAn array may contain:\\n\\n- Primitive values\\n\\n- Other arrays\\n\\nThe task therefore comes down to the traversal of a tree.\\nThe recursive approach\\n\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nWhat matters here\\n\\n- The check through `Array.isArray`\\n\\n- The base case is an ordinary value\\n\\n- The recursion naturally reflects the structure of the data\\n\\nThe complexity\\n\\n- The time complexity: O(n), where `n` is the total number of elements\\n\\n- The memory: it depends on the depth of the nesting (the call stack)\\n\\nConclusion\\nRecursion is the most readable and most universal way of counting the elements in nested arrays.","function countItems(arr) {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  for (const item of arr) {\\n    if (Array.isArray(item)) {\\n      count += countItems(item);\\n    } else {\\n      count++;\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return count;\\n}\\n\\ncountItems([1, [2, 3], [4, [5]]]); // 5"],[1271,"The task is solved either by recursion or iteratively through a stack. Recursion is simpler, but it can lead to a `stack overflow` with deep nesting. The iterative approach with a stack of your own is safer and is suitable for an infinite or a very deep structure. In practice the choice depends on the limitations on the depth of the data.","Why ordinary recursion can be dangerous\\n\\n- Every call takes up a place in the call stack\\n\\n- With a great depth a `RangeError` arises\\n\\n- The depth of the data is not always controllable\\n\\nThe iterative solution through a stack\\n\\nThe advantages of the iterative approach\\n\\n- There is no risk of an overflow of the call stack\\n\\n- Control over the memory\\n\\n- It is suitable for an \\"infinite\\" depth\\n\\nWhen recursion is enough\\n\\n- The depth is limited\\n\\n- The code has to be as readable as possible\\n\\n- There are no strict requirements of fault tolerance\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor an unknown or potentially infinite nesting it is better to use the iterative approach with a stack, and recursion only with a guaranteed depth.","function countItems(arr) {\\n  let count = 0;\\n  const stack = [...arr];\\n\\n  while (stack.length) {\\n    const item = stack.pop();\\n\\n    if (Array.isArray(item)) {\\n      stack.push(...item);\\n    } else {\\n      count++;\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return count;\\n}"],[1272,"Scripts can block the rendering, because the browser has to stop the parsing of the HTML, load and carry out the JavaScript. This is necessary, since a script may change the DOM or the CSS. While a synchronous script has not been carried out, the browser does not carry on building the DOM and cannot render the page correctly. The `defer` and `async` attributes let you reduce or remove that blocking.","JavaScript takes part directly in the forming of a page, so the browser treats it as a resource potentially dangerous for the render.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: A render-blocking script is a script that stops the parsing of the HTML and delays the painting of the page until the moment of its loading and execution.\\nWhy the browser blocks the parsing\\nThe browser does not know in advance what the script will do:\\n\\n- it may change the DOM (`document.write`, `appendChild`)\\n\\n- it may read the styles and the dimensions of the elements\\n\\n- it may add new styles or scripts\\n\\nBecause of that the browser is obliged to carry the script out strictly in the place where it is met with.\\nThe behaviour of an ordinary `<script>`\\n\\n- The parsing of the HTML stops.\\n\\n- The script is loaded (if it is external).\\n\\n- The script is carried out.\\n\\n- The parsing of the HTML continues.\\n\\nHow `defer` and `async` change the situation\\n\\n- `defer`\\n\\n- The HTML is parsed without a stop.\\n\\n- The script is carried out after the DOM has been built.\\n\\n- The order of the execution is preserved.\\n\\n- `async`\\n\\n- The HTML is parsed without a stop.\\n\\n- The script is carried out right after it is loaded.\\n\\n- The order of the execution is not guaranteed.\\n\\nThe practical effect\\n\\n- Synchronous scripts -> a delay in the `FCP`, a \\"white screen\\".\\n\\n- `defer` -> the safe variant for most applications.\\n\\n- `async` -> good for independent scripts (metrics, advertising).\\n\\nConclusion: scripts block the rendering because the browser is obliged to carry them out before continuing the parsing. The use of `defer` and `async` is the key to speeding a page\'s loading up.","<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n<script src=\\"analytics.js\\" async><\/script>"],[1273,"Scripts are worth loading at the end of the `body` when they do not use `defer` or `async`. In that case the browser first builds the DOM and displays the content, and only then carries out the JavaScript. This reduces the delay of the first paint and lowers the risk of errors caused by missing elements in the DOM. Such an approach is especially relevant for simple pages and old projects.","The placement of scripts is a simple but effective way of managing the loading.\\nThe idea of the approach\\nIf a script is at the end of the `body`, the browser:\\n\\n- First builds almost the whole DOM.\\n\\n- Carries out the first paint.\\n\\n- Only then loads and carries out the JavaScript.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nIn that case `document.getElementById(\'app\')` is guaranteed to exist.\\nWhen this is justified\\n\\n- there is no ability to use `defer`\\n\\n- a simple site without a complex build\\n\\n- legacy code or third-party scripts\\n\\n- the problem of the blocking has to be solved fast\\n\\nThe limitations of the approach\\n\\n- it does not solve the problem of the order of the loading of several scripts\\n\\n- it does not give the flexibility that `defer` does\\n\\n- it scales worse in large applications\\n\\nThe modern alternative\\nToday what is more often used is:\\n\\nThis gives the same effect, but more cleanly and more predictably.\\nConclusion: the loading of scripts at the end of the `body` is a workable way of reducing the blocking of the rendering, but in modern applications `defer` more often replaces it.","<body>\\n  <div id=\\"app\\"></div>\\n\\n  <script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script>\\n</body>\\n\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[1274,"`NaN` means \\"not a number\\" and by the specification is equal to nothing, itself included. This was done to reflect errors of computation rather than a specific value. Any comparison of `NaN` through `==` or `===` therefore returns `false`. To check for `NaN` special methods are used.","The behaviour of `NaN` seems strange, but it is deliberate.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: NaN is a special numeric value denoting an incorrect result of a computation.\\nWhy it was conceived this way\\n`NaN` does not represent a specific number but signals an error:\\n\\n- the division of a string by a number\\n\\n- the result of `0 / 0`\\n\\n- incorrect mathematical operations\\n\\nIf `NaN === NaN` were `true`, that could hide errors in computations.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow to check for NaN correctly\\n\\n- `Number.isNaN(value)` — the recommended way\\n\\n- the global `isNaN()` — it coerces the types and can be misleading\\n\\nConclusion: `NaN !== NaN` because `NaN` is a marker of an error rather than an ordinary value, and a comparison with it is always false.","const a = Number(\'abc\');\\n\\na === a; // false\\n\\nNumber.isNaN(NaN);      // true\\nNumber.isNaN(\'text\');  // false"],[1275,"In non-strict comparison `==` JavaScript tries to coerce the operands into one type. The rules depend on the types of the values: strings may turn into numbers, `null` and `undefined` are considered equal to one another, and objects are coerced into primitives. These conversions are not always obvious, because of which the comparison may behave unexpectedly. It is precisely for that reason that in most cases it is recommended that you use `===`.","Non-strict comparison obeys formal but complex rules.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: Type coercion is JavaScript\'s automatic conversion of types in the carrying out of operations, `==` included.\\nThe general logic of the `==` comparison\\nBefore the comparison JavaScript may:\\n\\n- Coerce a string into a number.\\n\\n- Coerce an object into a primitive.\\n\\n- Handle `null` and `undefined` in a particular way.\\n\\nThe typical rules\\n\\n- A string and a number:\\n\\n- `null` and `undefined`:\\n\\n- An object and a primitive:\\n\\nThe object is first converted into a primitive.\\nWhy this is dangerous\\n\\n- the comparison looks \\"logical\\" but works implicitly\\n\\n- it is easy to make a mistake in conditions\\n\\n- the code is harder to read and maintain\\n\\nThe recommendation\\nUse `===` unless there is an explicit reason to rely on the coercion of types.\\n\\nConclusion: with `==` JavaScript automatically coerces the types by a set of rules, which makes the behaviour flexible but potentially dangerous.","\'5\' == 5; // true\\n\\nnull == undefined; // true\\n\\n[1] == 1; // true\\n\\n\'5\' === 5; // false"],[1276,"In a browser the global context of execution is the `window` object. Every global variable and function becomes its property. Code written outside functions and modules is carried out precisely in that context. Through the global context the browser provides access to APIs such as `document`, `location` and `setTimeout`.","To understand the behaviour of code, it matters to know where it \\"lives\\".\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: The global context of execution is the base context in which code outside functions is carried out, and which is created when the page is loaded.\\nWhat is in the browser\'s global context\\nIn a browser the global object is `window`:\\n\\n- the global variables (`var a = 1`)\\n\\n- the global functions\\n\\n- the Web APIs (`document`, `history`, `location`)\\n\\n- the timers (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`)\\n\\nThe particulars of `let` and `const`\\nVariables declared through `let` and `const`:\\n\\n- are in the global scope,\\n\\n- but do not become properties of `window`.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- an understanding of conflicts of names\\n\\n- work with third-party scripts\\n\\n- the diagnosis of leaks of global state\\n\\nConclusion: in a browser the global context of execution is connected with the `window` object, which holds the global data and provides the Web APIs.","var x = 10;\\nconsole.log(window.x); // 10\\n\\nlet y = 5;\\nconsole.log(window.y); // undefined"],[1277,"In a browser the global object is `window`, and in Node.js it is `global`. In a browser the code is oriented towards work with the DOM and the Web APIs, and in Node.js towards the file system and server-side tasks. Besides that, in Node.js every file has its own module context rather than a shared global scope as in a browser. This affects the scope of variables and the behaviour of `this`.","JavaScript is one, but the runtime environment differs.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: The global object is the top-level object through which the environment of the execution of JavaScript is available.\\nThe browser\\nIn a browser:\\n\\n- the global object is `window`\\n\\n- the DOM is available (`document`)\\n\\n- the global `this` points to `window`\\n\\nNode.js\\nIn Node.js:\\n\\n- the global object is `global`\\n\\n- there is no DOM\\n\\n- every file is a separate module\\n\\nVariables declared in a file do not land in `global` directly.\\nThe practical significance\\n\\n- code for a browser cannot be run directly in Node.js\\n\\n- libraries often make checks of the environment\\n\\n- it matters for SSR and for universal JavaScript\\n\\nConclusion: the global context depends on the environment: in a browser it is `window`, in Node.js it is `global`, and the rules of scope differ.","console.log(this === window); // true\\n\\nconsole.log(this === global); // false"],[1278,"A linked list can be implemented by means of objects, in which every element holds a value and a reference to the next element. The initial element is called the `head`. To add a new element you have to create a new node and change the reference of the previous one. Such a structure is convenient for frequent insertions and deletions.","A linked list is a simple but instructive data structure.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: A linked list is a data structure consisting of nodes, in which every node holds a value and a reference to the next node.\\nThe basic structure of a node\\n\\nThe creation of a list\\n\\nThe addition of an element\\n\\nWhen this is useful\\n\\n- frequent insertions and deletions\\n\\n- learning to work with references\\n\\n- an understanding of the principles of data structures\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- slow access by index\\n\\n- more memory than an array\\n\\nConclusion: a linked list in JavaScript is implemented through objects and references between them, and it is useful for an understanding of the basic algorithms and data structures.","function Node(value) {\\n  this.value = value;\\n  this.next = null;\\n}\\n\\nconst head = new Node(1);\\nhead.next = new Node(2);\\nhead.next.next = new Node(3);\\n\\nfunction append(head, value) {\\n  let current = head;\\n\\n  while (current.next) {\\n    current = current.next;\\n  }\\n\\n  current.next = new Node(value);\\n}"],[1279,"Primitives (`number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `bigint` and `symbol`, for example) are passed into a function as a value. This means that the function\'s parameter receives a copy of the value rather than a \\"reference\\" to the original variable. If you change the parameter inside the function, only the local copy will change. Outside, the original variable will remain as it was.","JavaScript passes arguments by value, but it matters to understand that the \\"value\\" of a primitive and of an object are different. For a primitive it is the value itself (a number, a string and so on), so the function cannot \\"reach\\" the outer variable and change it directly.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: Pass-by-value is when a function\'s parameter receives a copy of the value passed, and further changes to the parameter do not affect the original variable.\\nWhat happens on the call of a function\\nWhen you call a function:\\n\\n- The value of the argument is evaluated.\\n\\n- A parameter is created (a local variable of the function).\\n\\n- A copy of that value is written into the parameter.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- `a` remains `10`, because `x` is a separate local variable.\\n\\n- `x = x + 1` is the reassignment of the local parameter.\\n\\nWhy this matters in real code\\n\\n- Predictability\\n\\n- Functions working with primitives are by default \\"safer\\": they do not change the external state unless they explicitly return a new value.\\n\\n- The immutable style\\n\\n- In a UI (in React included) it is often more convenient \\"not to mutate\\" but to return a new value.\\n\\nThe frequent confusion: \\"passing by reference\\"\\nSometimes people say that \\"a primitive is passed by value, an object by reference\\". More precisely it is like this:\\n\\n- JavaScript always passes by value.\\n\\n- For objects that value is a reference (an identifier) to the object.\\n\\nBecause of that the behaviour of objects looks \\"as if by reference\\", but technically it is \\"a copy of the reference\\".\\nConclusion: primitives are passed as a copy of the value, so the reassignment of a parameter inside a function does not affect the variable outside.","function inc(x) {\\n  x = x + 1;\\n  return x;\\n}\\n\\nlet a = 10;\\nconst b = inc(a);\\n\\nconsole.log(a); // 10\\nconsole.log(b); // 11"],[1280,"Because inside the function you are changing not the outer variable but the local parameter, which contains a copy of the value. A primitive cannot be \\"changed inside\\" — a new value can only be assigned to a variable. When you do `x = ...`, you are reassigning the parameter `x` rather than the original variable. Nothing therefore changes outside.","Here it matters to distinguish two actions: changing a variable\'s value and trying to change \\"the primitive itself\\". Primitives have no \\"internal state\\" that can be mutated — they have only a value, and a variable can point to one value or to another.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: Reassignment is when a variable is given a new value and begins to hold it instead of the old one.\\nThe analysis with an example\\n\\nWhat happened:\\n\\n- In `setZero(n)` the value of `n` was evaluated (that is `5`).\\n\\n- The parameter `x` received a copy of `5`.\\n\\n- `x = 0` changed only the local variable `x`.\\n\\n- The variable `n` outside the function was not touched.\\n\\nWhy \\"inside a function\\" you cannot change an outer primitive\\n\\n- The function has no access to the variable `n` itself; it sees only the value passed at the call.\\n\\n- To change the outer variable you have to:\\n\\n- return a new value and assign it outside, or\\n\\n- change an object (if an object has been passed), or\\n\\n- use a closure (if it is a variable from an outer scope rather than an argument).\\n\\nAn example with a return:\\n\\nThe practical benefit\\n\\n- such functions are easier to test\\n\\n- there are fewer \\"hidden\\" side effects\\n\\n- it is simpler to maintain code in large projects\\n\\nConclusion: the change of a primitive \\"inside a function\\" is in fact the reassignment of the local parameter, so the outer variable does not change.","function setZero(x) {\\n  x = 0;\\n}\\n\\nlet n = 5;\\nsetZero(n);\\n\\nconsole.log(n); // 5\\n\\nfunction setZero(x) {\\n  return 0;\\n}\\n\\nlet n = 5;\\nn = setZero(n);\\n\\nconsole.log(n); // 0"],[1281,"If you pass an object into a function, you can change its properties inside the function, and those changes will be visible outside. This happens because the parameter receives a copy of the reference to one and the same object. While you are changing the properties (`obj.count++`, for example), you are working with that same object. But if you reassign the parameter itself (`obj = {}`, for example), the outer object will not change.","This question helps you understand an important model: the function receives a copy of the reference to the object, so both sides \\"look at\\" one and the same object in memory.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: The change of an object (mutation) is a change of its internal state (its properties, for example) without the creation of a new object.\\nAn example: the change of a property inside a function\\n\\nWhy this works:\\n\\n- `state` holds a reference to the object `{ count: 0 }`.\\n\\n- The value of that reference (a copy of the reference) is passed into the function.\\n\\n- The function\'s parameter points to the same object.\\n\\n- `state.count += 1` changes the object, so the result is visible outside.\\n\\nAn important difference: the change of a property vs the reassignment of the parameter\\nThe change of a property changes the same object:\\n\\nThe reassignment of the parameter does not change the outer object:\\n\\nHow to use this \\"correctly\\"\\nHere there are two widespread approaches — the choice depends on the style of the code and on the requirements.\\n\\n- Imperatively (mutate the object)\\n\\n- it is convenient in simple code\\n\\n- it is fast and without superfluous allocations\\n\\n- but there is a risk of unexpected side effects\\n\\n- Functionally (return a new object)\\n\\n- it is more predictable, especially in a UI\\n\\n- it is simpler to track changes and make comparisons\\n\\n- it is often used in React/Redux approaches\\n\\nAn example without mutations:\\n\\nA practical remark for React\\n\\n- In React state is usually updated without mutations, so that the comparison of references helps you work out that the data has changed.\\n\\n- But local objects (not state) can be mutated if that does not affect the render logic.\\n\\nConclusion: to \\"change data through a function\\", pass an object and change its properties — the changes will be visible outside, because the function receives a copy of the reference to one and the same object. If predictability matters (in React, for example), a new object is more often returned instead of mutations.","function incrementCounter(state) {\\n  state.count += 1;\\n}\\n\\nconst state = { count: 0 };\\nincrementCounter(state);\\n\\nconsole.log(state.count); // 1\\n\\nfunction setName(user) {\\n  user.name = \'Ann\';\\n}\\n\\nconst u = { name: \'Bob\' };\\nsetName(u);\\n\\nconsole.log(u.name); // \'Ann\'\\n\\nfunction replaceUser(user) {\\n  user = { name: \'Ann\' }; // a new object, but the reference is only in the local parameter\\n}\\n\\nconst u = { name: \'Bob\' };\\nreplaceUser(u);\\n\\nconsole.log(u.name); // \'Bob\'\\n\\nfunction incrementCounter(state) {\\n  return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };\\n}\\n\\nlet state = { count: 0 };\\nstate = incrementCounter(state);"],[1282,"With the use of `var` in a loop every asynchronous callback will refer to one and the same variable. By the moment the asynchronous code is carried out the loop will already have finished, and the variable will have its final value. As a result, instead of the different values expected, one and the same one is printed. This happens because of the function rather than block scope of `var`.","The problem arises because of the combination of a closure and the scope of `var`.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: A closure is the situation in which a function \\"remembers\\" the variables from an outer scope and carries on having access to them later.\\nWhat `var` does in a loop\\n\\n- `var` has function rather than block scope\\n\\n- in the loop one variable is created rather than a new one on every iteration\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nWhat will happen:\\n\\n- The loop will be carried out in full.\\n\\n- `i` will become equal to `3`.\\n\\n- The asynchronous callbacks will be carried out later.\\n\\n- All of them will print `3`.\\n\\nWhy this is unexpected\\nA developer intuitively expects:\\n\\nbut gets:\\n\\nWhere this is most often met with\\n\\n- `setTimeout` / `setInterval`\\n\\n- event handlers in loops\\n\\n- promises created inside a loop\\n\\nConclusion: with the use of `var` in asynchronous loops every callback shares one variable, so they get one and the same final value.","for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(i);\\n  }, 100);\\n}\\n\\n0\\n1\\n2\\n\\n3\\n3\\n3"],[1283,"The simplest way is to use `let` instead of `var`, since `let` creates a new variable for every iteration of the loop. You can also use an IIFE or pass the value into a function explicitly. These approaches let every asynchronous callback work with \\"its own\\" value. In modern code `let` is preferable.","The solution always comes down to one thing: create a separate scope for every iteration.\\n1) The use of `let` (the recommended way)\\n\\nWhy it works:\\n\\n- `let` has block scope\\n\\n- on every iteration a new variable `i` is created\\n\\n2) The use of an IIFE (an approach becoming outdated, but an instructive one)\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n- the value of `i` is copied into the parameter `value`\\n\\n- every callback closes over its own value\\n\\n3) The passing of the value into an asynchronous function\\n\\nThe practical recommendation\\n\\n- Use `let` by default\\n\\n- Leave `var` only for legacy code\\n\\n- an understanding of IIFEs matters for reading old projects\\n\\nConclusion: the correct output in an asynchronous loop is achieved by the creation of a separate scope for every iteration, most often by means of `let`.","for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(i);\\n  }, 100);\\n}\\n\\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  (function (value) {\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      console.log(value);\\n    }, 100);\\n  })(i);\\n}\\n\\nfunction logLater(value) {\\n  setTimeout(() => console.log(value), 100);\\n}\\n\\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  logLater(i);\\n}"],[1284,"In the comparison of an object with a number the `==` operator tries to coerce the object into a primitive. For that the `valueOf` and `toString` methods are called. If the result of the coercion does not coincide with the number, the comparison returns `false`. The behaviour may look unexpected, but it is strictly defined by the specification.","When an object takes part in a comparison, JavaScript first tries to turn it into a primitive.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: ToPrimitive is the algorithm of the coercion of an object into a primitive value before operations such as `==`.\\nHow the comparison proceeds\\nIn the expression:\\n\\nJavaScript does the following:\\n\\n- It sees that one operand is an object.\\n\\n- It starts the coercion of the object into a primitive.\\n\\n- It compares the resulting primitive with the number.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhy:\\n\\n- `obj.valueOf()` -> returns the object itself\\n\\n- `obj.toString()` -> `\\"[object Object]\\"`\\n\\n- the string `\\"[object Object]\\"` is not equal to `1`\\n\\nAn example in which the comparison will be true\\n\\nWhy this is considered dangerous\\n\\n- the behaviour is non-obvious\\n\\n- it is easy to get a bug in conditions\\n\\n- the code is hard to read and maintain\\n\\nThe practical recommendation\\n\\n- avoid `==` in the comparison of objects\\n\\n- use `===`\\n\\n- coerce the types explicitly when it is necessary\\n\\nConclusion: an object is not equal to a number with `==` because JavaScript first coerces the object into a primitive, and the result of that coercion most often does not coincide with the number.","obj == 1\\n\\nconst obj = { value: 1 };\\n\\nobj == 1; // false\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  valueOf() {\\n    return 1;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nobj == 1; // true"],[1285,"In the coercion of an object into a primitive JavaScript first tries to call `valueOf`, and if it returns no primitive, it calls `toString`. This order is applied by default for the numeric context. If the result is still not a primitive, an error is thrown. The behaviour is strictly described in the specification.","When an object takes part in operations of comparison, addition or the conversion of types, JavaScript starts a special algorithm.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: ToPrimitive is the algorithm of the conversion of an object into a primitive value before the carrying out of operations.\\nThe basic order of the calls\\nBy default (the numeric context):\\n\\n- `obj.valueOf()` is called\\n\\n- If the result is a primitive, it is used\\n\\n- Otherwise `obj.toString()` is called\\n\\n- If the result is a primitive, it is used\\n\\n- Otherwise a `TypeError` is thrown\\n\\nAn example\\n\\n`valueOf` returned an object, so JavaScript moved on to `toString`.\\nThe context matters\\nFor the string context the order can change:\\n\\nIn that case JavaScript will prefer `toString`.\\nThe modern mechanism\\nIf an object has the method:\\n\\nit is called before `valueOf` and `toString` and completely governs the coercion.\\nConclusion: by default JavaScript first calls `valueOf` and then `toString`, but the order can depend on the context and be overridden through `Symbol.toPrimitive`.","const obj = {\\n  valueOf() {\\n    return {};\\n  },\\n  toString() {\\n    return \'10\';\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nNumber(obj); // 10\\n\\nString(obj);\\n\\nobj[Symbol.toPrimitive]"],[1286,"Yes, the coercion of types on an object can be overridden. For that `Symbol.toPrimitive` is used, and also the `valueOf` and `toString` methods. When `Symbol.toPrimitive` is present, it is precisely that which has the highest priority. This lets you control precisely how an object behaves in different contexts.","JavaScript provides several levels of control over the coercion of types.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: Symbol.toPrimitive is a special method that defines how an object is converted into a primitive.\\nThe priority of the mechanisms\\nJavaScript uses the following order:\\n\\n- `obj[Symbol.toPrimitive]`\\n\\n- `valueOf()`\\n\\n- `toString()`\\n\\nAn example with Symbol.toPrimitive\\n\\nWhen this can be useful\\n\\n- logging and debugging\\n\\n- value objects (money, dimensions, units of measurement)\\n\\n- a convenient API for libraries\\n\\nWhy it is used with care\\n\\n- it worsens the readability\\n\\n- it can be misleading\\n\\n- it is rarely what other developers expect\\n\\nConclusion: yes, the coercion of types can be overridden, and the highest-priority way is `Symbol.toPrimitive`, but it is worth applying it knowingly.","const user = {\\n  name: \'Alex\',\\n  age: 30,\\n  [Symbol.toPrimitive](hint) {\\n    if (hint === \'number\') return this.age;\\n    if (hint === \'string\') return this.name;\\n    return `${this.name} (${this.age})`;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nNumber(user); // 30\\nString(user); // \'Alex\'\\nuser + \'\'; // \'Alex (30)\'"],[1287,"`then` and `finally` are carried out after the synchronous code because they are placed in the queue of microtasks. JavaScript first carries out all of the synchronous code in the current call stack. Only after that does the event loop begin to handle the microtasks. The handlers of promises are therefore always carried out \\"a little later\\".","To understand this behaviour you have to analyse JavaScript\'s model of execution.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: The event loop is the mechanism that manages the execution of synchronous code, microtasks and macrotasks.\\nHow the code is carried out\\nJavaScript follows a strict order:\\n\\n- All of the synchronous code is carried out (the call stack).\\n\\n- Then every microtask is carried out.\\n\\n- Then the next macrotask is taken.\\n\\nWhere then and finally are\\n\\n- `Promise.then`\\n\\n- `Promise.catch`\\n\\n- `Promise.finally`\\n\\nAll of them are added to the microtask queue.\\nAn example\\n\\nThe order of the output:\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- it explains the \\"unexpected\\" order of the `console.log`s\\n\\n- it helps you debug asynchronous code\\n\\n- it is critical for React, where there are many promises and effects\\n\\nA frequent mistake\\nThe expectation that a `then` will be carried out \\"right after the resolve\\" within the same synchronous block.\\nConclusion: `then` and `finally` are carried out after the synchronous code because they are handled as microtasks and are started by the event loop only after the current call stack has been cleared.","console.log(\'start\');\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\'then\');\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\'end\');\\n\\nstart\\nend\\nthen"],[1288,"To determine the order of the output of `console.log` you have to understand the working of the event loop. First the synchronous code from the call stack is always carried out. Then every microtask is carried out (`then`, `catch` and `finally`, for example). Only after that are the macrotasks handled (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`, events). Knowing which queue an operation belongs to, you can predict the order of the logs precisely.","The order of the execution in JavaScript is not random — it is strictly regulated by the model of the event loop.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: The event loop is the mechanism that manages the queues of tasks and determines what code will be carried out and when.\\nThe main participants\\nBefore the analysis of any example it is useful to remember three levels of execution:\\n\\n- The call stack\\n\\n- all of the synchronous code\\n\\n- The microtask queue\\n\\n- `Promise.then`\\n\\n- `Promise.catch`\\n\\n- `Promise.finally`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- The macrotask queue\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- events\\n\\n- `setImmediate` (in Node.js)\\n\\nThe algorithm for determining the order\\nTo determine the order of the `console.log`s, act step by step:\\n\\n- Write out all of the synchronous code — it will be carried out first.\\n\\n- Find the microtasks and remember the order in which they were placed.\\n\\n- Find the macrotasks.\\n\\n- Once the synchronous code has finished:\\n\\n- every microtask is carried out first,\\n\\n- then one macrotask,\\n\\n- then the microtasks again, and so on.\\n\\nAn example with an analysis\\n\\nThe analysis:\\n\\n- Synchronously:\\n\\n- `A`\\n\\n- `D`\\n\\n- The microtasks:\\n\\n- `C`\\n\\n- The macrotasks:\\n\\n- `B`\\n\\nThe resulting output:\\n\\nThe frequent traps\\n\\n- `setTimeout(fn, 0)` does not mean \\"carry it out at once\\"\\n\\n- microtasks can \\"strangle\\" macrotasks if there are too many of them\\n\\n- `async/await` also uses promises and microtasks internally\\n\\nConclusion: to determine the order of the output of the logs, you have to separate the synchronous code, the microtasks and the macrotasks strictly and apply the rules of the event loop step by step.","console.log(\'A\');\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'B\');\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\'C\');\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\'D\');\\n\\nA\\nD\\nC\\nB"],[1289,"`NaN` is the only value in JavaScript that is not equal to itself. A check of the form `value !== value` therefore lets you determine `NaN`. Such a way works without the use of `Number.isNaN`. It is less readable, however, and is rarely used in application code.","Although `Number.isNaN` is usually used, it is useful to understand how `NaN` can be determined \\"by hand\\".\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: NaN is the only value in JavaScript for which the expression `x !== x` returns `true`.\\nThe implementation of the function\\n\\nWhy this works\\n\\n- any normal value is equal to itself\\n\\n- `NaN !== NaN` is always `true`\\n\\n- there are no other such values in the language\\n\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nThe limitations of the approach\\n\\n- it works only for `NaN`\\n\\n- it is less obvious to read\\n\\n- it is less suitable for production code\\n\\nConclusion: the check `value !== value` is a valid way of determining `NaN`, founded on its unique property, but `Number.isNaN` is usually preferable.","function isNaNValue(value) {\\n  return value !== value;\\n}\\n\\nisNaNValue(NaN); // true\\nisNaNValue(5);   // false\\nisNaNValue(\'a\'); // false"],[1290,"Because `NaN` by the IEEE 754 standard is not equal to any value, itself included. This behaviour is deliberately written into the specification. Every other value in JavaScript is equal to itself. The comparison of a value with itself therefore lets you reliably detect `NaN`.","The behaviour of `NaN` seems strange, but it is logical from the point of view of the standard for floating-point numbers.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: IEEE 754 is the standard for the representation of floating-point numbers, used in JavaScript.\\nThe mathematical idea\\n`NaN` means \\"the result is not defined\\". It does not represent a specific number, so:\\n\\n- `NaN == NaN` -> `false`\\n\\n- `NaN === NaN` -> `false`\\n\\nThe connection with the check\\nAny value `x` in JavaScript:\\n\\n- `x === x` -> `true`\\n\\n- except `NaN`\\n\\nFrom that the check arises:\\n\\nThe practical value\\n\\n- it lets you implement the check without the built-in functions\\n\\n- it is used in low-level code\\n\\n- it is useful for an understanding of the language rather than as the main device\\n\\nConclusion: the comparison of an argument with itself works for the determination of `NaN` because `NaN` is the only value not equal to itself.","value !== value"],[1291,"`Object.is` and `===` are similar, but they behave differently in two cases: in the comparison of `NaN` and of `+0` / `-0`. `Object.is(NaN, NaN)` returns `true`, while `NaN === NaN` returns `false`. Also `Object.is(+0, -0)` returns `false`, whereas `+0 === -0` returns `true`. In every other case the behaviour is identical.","`Object.is` was added in order to give a more \\"precise\\" comparison of values.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: Object.is is a method of comparison that checks values by the SameValue algorithm.\\nThe key differences from `===`\\nThere are exactly two differences.\\n1) NaN\\n\\n2) The signed zero\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- for low-level comparisons\\n\\n- for the correct working of numerical algorithms\\n\\n- for libraries and frameworks\\n\\nThe connection with React\\nReact uses the comparison of references (`===`) for optimisation, but in some places (`Object.is` in the hooks, for example) it is precisely `Object.is` that is used, for the correct handling of `NaN`.\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- `===` — by default in application code\\n\\n- `Object.is` — when the edge cases matter\\n\\nConclusion: `Object.is` differs from `===` only in the comparison of `NaN` and `+0 / -0`, but it is precisely those differences that make it a more precise tool of comparison.","Object.is(NaN, NaN); // true\\nNaN === NaN;         // false\\n\\nObject.is(+0, -0); // false\\n+0 === -0;         // true"],[1292,"First all of the synchronous code is carried out. Then the microtasks are carried out, the handlers of `Promise`s included. After that the macrotasks are carried out, such as `setTimeout`. This order repeats on every iteration of the event loop. A `Promise` is therefore usually carried out before a `setTimeout`.","JavaScript is carried out in one thread, but it can handle asynchronous operations.\\nThe definition\\nThe event loop is the mechanism that manages the execution of code, the queues of tasks and the call stack.\\nThe order of the execution\\nBefore moving on to the asynchronous code, JavaScript always carries out the synchronous instructions.\\n\\n- The synchronous code\\n\\n- It is carried out at once in the call stack\\n\\n- The microtask queue\\n\\n- `Promise.then`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- The macrotask queue\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- events\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe result of the execution:\\n\\n- `A`\\n\\n- `D`\\n\\n- `C`\\n\\n- `B`\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the queues of tasks helps you predict the order of the execution of code and avoid errors in asynchronous logic.","console.log(\\"A\\");\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"B\\"));\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"C\\"));\\n\\nconsole.log(\\"D\\");"],[1293,"The Web APIs let the browser carry out asynchronous operations outside the main JavaScript thread. Timers, network requests and events are handled by the browser rather than by the JavaScript engine. Once the operation has finished, the callback is placed in the corresponding queue of tasks. The event loop then decides when to carry that code out.","The JavaScript engine in itself cannot carry out asynchronous operations.\\nWhat the Web APIs are\\nThe Web APIs are the browser\'s interfaces, which provide capabilities not available to pure JavaScript.\\nExamples of Web APIs\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `fetch`\\n\\n- DOM Events\\n\\n- `WebSocket`\\n\\nHow they take part in the event loop\\n\\n- The asynchronous operation is passed to a Web API\\n\\n- JavaScript carries on with the execution of the synchronous code\\n\\n- Once the operation has finished, the callback lands in the queue of tasks\\n\\n- The event loop adds it to the call stack\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe timer is handled by a Web API, and the function will be carried out later through the event loop.\\nConclusion\\nThe Web APIs extend JavaScript\'s capabilities and let asynchronous behaviour be implemented without the blocking of the main thread.","setTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"Done\\");\\n}, 1000);"],[1294,"`MutationObserver` lets you track changes in the DOM without constant checks. It reacts to the addition, the deletion or the change of nodes. `queueMicrotask` lets you add a task to the microtask queue by hand. Both mechanisms are closely connected with the event loop and are carried out before the macrotasks.","Work with the DOM and with asynchronicity often requires precise control of the moment of the execution of code.\\nMutationObserver\\nMutationObserver is an API for observing changes in the DOM tree.\\nWhat it can track\\n\\n- The addition and deletion of nodes\\n\\n- The change of attributes\\n\\n- The change of textual content\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe callback will be called asynchronously after the changes in the DOM.\\nqueueMicrotask\\nqueueMicrotask is a function for adding a task to the microtask queue.\\nThe particulars\\n\\n- It is carried out after the current synchronous code\\n\\n- It is carried out before `setTimeout`\\n\\n- It is analogous to `Promise.then`, but without the creation of a Promise\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe connection with the event loop\\n\\n- The DOM changes are recorded\\n\\n- MutationObserver adds a task to the microtask queue\\n\\n- The event loop carries out the microtask before the macrotasks\\n\\nConclusion\\n`MutationObserver` is suitable for reactions to changes in the DOM, and `queueMicrotask` for the precise management of the order of the execution of asynchronous code.","const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {\\n  console.log(mutations);\\n});\\n\\nobserver.observe(document.body, {\\n  childList: true,\\n  subtree: true,\\n});\\n\\nqueueMicrotask(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"microtask\\");\\n});"],[1295,"Deep copying creates a new copy of an object with all of its nested structures. One of the simple ways is serialisation through JSON, but it has limitations. The modern and safe way is `structuredClone`. You can also implement recursive copying by hand. The choice of the method depends on the types of the data.","Shallow copying copies only the references, so changes affect the original object.\\nThe definition\\nDeep copying is the creation of a new object with all of its nested values without shared references.\\nThe main ways\\n\\n- JSON serialisation\\n\\nIt is suitable only for simple data.\\n\\n- structuredClone\\n\\nIt supports most of the built-in types.\\n\\n- Recursive copying\\n\\n- It is used for complete control of the logic\\n\\n- It requires the handling of circular references\\n\\nThe limitations of the methods\\n\\n- JSON does not copy functions or `undefined`\\n\\n- structuredClone is not available in very old browsers\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor modern applications it is preferable to use `structuredClone`, and JSON only for simple data structures.","const copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj));\\n\\nconst copy = structuredClone(obj);"],[1296,"JSON supports a limited set of data types. In serialisation functions, `undefined` and symbols are lost. Some types are converted into strings or are ignored. This is connected with the fact that JSON is intended for the transfer of data rather than of logic.","JSON is a textual format for the exchange of data with strict rules.\\nWhat types JSON supports\\n\\n- `string`\\n\\n- `number`\\n\\n- `boolean`\\n\\n- `null`\\n\\n- `object`\\n\\n- `array`\\n\\nWhat data is lost\\n\\n- Functions\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `Symbol`\\n\\n- Circular references\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhy this happens\\nJSON was created as a format for the transfer of data between systems, so it supports only universal primitives.\\nConclusion\\nJSON is suitable for the holding and the transfer of data, but not for the complex structures of JavaScript objects.","const obj = {\\n  a: 1,\\n  b: undefined,\\n  c: () => {},\\n};\\n\\nJSON.stringify(obj); // {\\"a\\":1}"],[1297,"Throttle is a device that limits the frequency of the call of a function. The function is carried out no more often than the interval of time given. Every call between the intervals is ignored or combined. This is often used for the optimisation of performance.","When working with the browser\'s events a function may be called too often.\\nThe definition\\nThrottle is a technique limiting the execution of a function to one call in a particular span of time.\\nWhere throttle is applied\\n\\n- The handling of `scroll`\\n\\n- The handling of `resize`\\n\\n- The tracking of the movement of the mouse\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe difference from debounce\\n\\n- Throttle carries it out regularly\\n\\n- Debounce carries it out after a pause\\n\\nConclusion\\nThrottle is useful when it matters to handle events regularly but without overloading the application.","function throttle(fn, delay) {\\n  let isThrottled = false;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    if (isThrottled) return;\\n\\n    isThrottled = true;\\n    fn();\\n\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      isThrottled = false;\\n    }, delay);\\n  };\\n}"],[1298,"To optimise a search over large arrays of data you can use several approaches:\\n\\n- Make the search asynchronous, using Web Workers or setTimeout.\\n\\n- Split the data into pages (pagination) and load only the part you need.\\n\\n- Use search algorithms of a lower complexity, such as a binary search for sorted data.","When the data is too large and we try to handle all of it at once on the client, that can lead to a substantial load on the browser, especially on mobile devices. Here are several ways of solving this problem:\\n\\n- An asynchronous search:\\n\\n- The application of `Web Workers` lets you carry the search out in a background thread without blocking the main UI thread.\\n\\n- For smaller volumes you can use `setTimeout`, in order to divide the search into parts and update the UI gradually.\\n\\n- The pagination of the data:\\n\\n- Instead of loading all of the data at once, it can be split into pages. The user will work with only a small part of the data.\\n\\n- This not only speeds the search up but also substantially lowers the load on the browser.\\n\\n- The use of efficient search algorithms:\\n\\n- For sorted data you can use a binary search, which has a complexity of O(log n), unlike a linear search\'s O(n).\\n\\nConclusion:\\nFor large arrays of data it matters to optimise both the search and the loading of the data. Pagination, asynchronous approaches and efficient algorithms can substantially raise the performance.",null],[1299,"HTTP/2 improves the performance of requests thanks to the following particulars:\\n\\n- Multiplexing — it lets you send several requests and receive the responses over one connection.\\n\\n- The compression of the headers — it reduces the volume of the data transferred.\\n\\n- Server push — it lets the server send resources in advance, even if the client has not yet requested them.","HTTP/2 represents an improved version of the HTTP/1.1 protocol, with several main improvements:\\n\\n- Multiplexing:\\n\\n- In HTTP/1.1 every request created a separate connection, which led to excessive costs of the establishment of connections and their closing. HTTP/2 solves that by means of multiplexing, letting you send several requests and receive several responses over one TCP connection.\\n\\n- The compression of the headers:\\n\\n- In HTTP/1.1 the headers of every request were transferred as text, which added a lot of superfluous data. HTTP/2 uses the HPACK compression algorithm, which substantially reduces the size of the headers, thereby speeding the transfer of the data up.\\n\\n- Server push:\\n\\n- The server can send resources (images or styles, for example) that in its opinion will be necessary to the client, even if the client has not requested them yet. This lets the delay be reduced, since the resources are loaded in advance.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nHTTP/2 substantially raises the performance of web applications thanks to the optimisation of the transfer of data and the improvement of the work with connections.",null],[1300,"HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is a protocol for the transfer of data in a network. It is used for the exchange of information between a client and a server. HTTP supports various methods of request:\\n\\n- GET — the retrieval of data.\\n\\n- POST — the sending of data to the server.\\n\\n- PUT — the updating of data.\\n\\n- DELETE — the deletion of data.","HTTP is the main protocol for the exchange of data on the internet. It is used mainly for the loading of web pages, but also for interaction with APIs and other services. Here are the key methods that HTTP supports:\\n\\n- GET — it is used for the retrieval of data from the server. For example, when you go to a web page, your browser sends a GET request to the server for the retrieval of the HTML, the CSS and other resources.\\n\\n- POST — it is used for the sending of data to the server. For example, the sending of a form with a user\'s data (a name, a password and so on) to the server for handling.\\n\\n- PUT — it is used for the updating of existing data on the server. For example, in the editing of a record in a database.\\n\\n- DELETE — it is used for the deletion of data from the server. That can be the deletion of a record in a database or of a file.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nHTTP is the foundation for the working of web applications, and the methods GET, POST, PUT and DELETE are the main tools for interaction with a server.",null]]');export{e as default};